1
00:00:33,992 --> 00:00:35,732
Come closer.

2
00:00:37,079 --> 00:00:38,364
Closer.

3
00:00:39,248 --> 00:00:42,240
Because the more you think you see...

4
00:00:42,334 --> 00:00:44,746
...the easier it'll be to fool you.

5
00:00:46,380 --> 00:00:48,245
Because what is seeing?

6
00:00:48,382 --> 00:00:52,375
You're looking, but what you're really doing is
filtering, interpreting...

7
00:00:52,469 --> 00:00:54,425
...searching for meaning.

8
00:00:56,431 --> 00:00:57,671
My job?

9
00:00:57,766 --> 00:01:01,759
To take the most precious gift you give me:
your attention...

10
00:01:04,648 --> 00:01:06,684
...and use it against you.

11
00:01:07,609 --> 00:01:10,817
CHICAGO
MONDAY

12
00:01:11,863 --> 00:01:14,070
I'm going to go through these cards.

13
00:01:14,157 --> 00:01:15,647
I want you to pick one.

14
00:01:15,742 --> 00:01:17,152
Not this one. It's too obvious.

15
00:01:17,578 --> 00:01:18,863
Pay attention.

16
00:01:21,456 --> 00:01:22,741
That was too fast.
Let me go again.

17
00:01:22,833 --> 00:01:24,039
Ready?

18
00:01:24,126 --> 00:01:25,126
All right.

19
00:01:27,170 --> 00:01:28,956
- Did you pick one?
- Yes.

20
00:01:29,131 --> 00:01:31,042
- Are you thinking of one?
- Yes.

21
00:01:31,258 --> 00:01:32,964
Do you see it here?

22
00:01:33,571 --> 00:01:34,571
No.

23
00:01:34,636 --> 00:01:36,843
Because you're looking too closely.

24
00:01:36,930 --> 00:01:38,966
What have I been saying all night?

25
00:01:39,057 --> 00:01:40,513
The closer you look...

26
00:01:40,601 --> 00:01:42,137
The less you see.

27
00:02:02,414 --> 00:02:03,904
NEW ORLEANS

28
00:02:03,999 --> 00:02:05,284
TUESDAY

29
00:02:05,375 --> 00:02:07,787
Look into my eyes.

30
00:02:08,003 --> 00:02:10,039
And sleep. Good.

31
00:02:13,342 --> 00:02:14,548
And...

32
00:02:15,719 --> 00:02:18,301
If you can take this bill from me,
you get to keep it.

33
00:02:18,513 --> 00:02:20,344
Go ahead. Take it. Grab it.

34
00:02:20,557 --> 00:02:23,048
If you can say your name,
you get to keep it.

35
00:02:26,229 --> 00:02:28,641
All right. Stay there. Make an effort.

36
00:02:28,732 --> 00:02:31,098
I'm going to take a little peek...

37
00:02:31,193 --> 00:02:32,683
...inside your husband's mind.

38
00:02:32,819 --> 00:02:33,934
I'm seeing...

39
00:02:34,029 --> 00:02:36,020
- Don't tell me.
- Of course.

40
00:02:36,239 --> 00:02:38,230
The beach. Some drinks.

41
00:02:38,700 --> 00:02:39,860
Florida?

42
00:02:39,951 --> 00:02:41,441
It was a business trip.

43
00:02:41,620 --> 00:02:43,611
Yeah, that's what they call it. Business.

44
00:02:43,705 --> 00:02:46,572
The oldest profession in the world.

45
00:02:46,708 --> 00:02:47,914
You know what, honey? Let's--

46
00:02:48,043 --> 00:02:49,533
He can't move, Mack.

47
00:02:49,628 --> 00:02:51,038
You're thinking of
a woman's name.

48
00:02:51,254 --> 00:02:52,585
A, B, C...

49
00:02:52,673 --> 00:02:53,913
...D, E, F, G...

50
00:02:54,007 --> 00:02:55,122
...H, I, J--

51
00:02:55,258 --> 00:02:57,044
J? Jean. Jane. Janet.

52
00:02:57,260 --> 00:02:58,340
Who's Janet?

53
00:02:59,971 --> 00:03:01,177
Do you know Janet?

54
00:03:01,306 --> 00:03:02,546
She's not your best friend, is she?

55
00:03:03,934 --> 00:03:05,549
- Your sister?
- No, please.

56
00:03:05,644 --> 00:03:07,851
Your sister? Oh, my God.

57
00:03:07,979 --> 00:03:10,015
You didn't go away on business.

58
00:03:10,107 --> 00:03:11,847
You went away for Janet.

59
00:03:11,942 --> 00:03:13,227
Your wife's sister!

60
00:03:14,194 --> 00:03:15,900
We need to move over here...

61
00:03:15,987 --> 00:03:18,524
...because it looks like
the lady's getting a little upset.

62
00:03:18,615 --> 00:03:20,822
Want to make this go away?

63
00:03:20,909 --> 00:03:22,570
- Yes.
- Take out your wallet.

64
00:03:23,120 --> 00:03:24,576
Come on, take it out.

65
00:03:24,663 --> 00:03:25,948
Do you blackmail everyone like this?

66
00:03:26,081 --> 00:03:27,821
No, only the special ones.

67
00:03:27,916 --> 00:03:29,998
How much is in there?
Would $200 be all right?

68
00:03:30,085 --> 00:03:32,167
You know what? This is serious.
Let's make it $250.

69
00:03:32,254 --> 00:03:33,254
You're a thief.

70
00:03:33,338 --> 00:03:34,999
Yeah, sure.

71
00:03:35,757 --> 00:03:38,544
All right, and sleep!

72
00:03:38,635 --> 00:03:40,000
Now, when I snap my fingers...

73
00:03:40,095 --> 00:03:41,551
...you won't remember a thing.

74
00:03:41,638 --> 00:03:42,844
And you, Warren Beatty...

75
00:03:43,098 --> 00:03:45,885
...every time you see Janet
or think about her...

76
00:03:46,268 --> 00:03:48,725
...you'll picture me naked.

77
00:03:48,812 --> 00:03:49,892
And it isn't a pretty picture.

78
00:03:50,021 --> 00:03:51,021
Yeah.

79
00:03:51,106 --> 00:03:53,267
Wake up!

80
00:03:56,361 --> 00:03:58,852
We gave it our best shot...

81
00:03:58,947 --> 00:04:01,108
...but some people
just can't be hypnotized.

82
00:04:01,199 --> 00:04:03,485
- Did I do something wrong?
- Come on, honey.

83
00:04:03,618 --> 00:04:05,279
- No, you did fine.
- Let's go to dinner.

84
00:04:05,454 --> 00:04:06,614
Take care of her.

85
00:04:06,705 --> 00:04:08,445
I need to stop
at an ATM.

86
00:04:17,340 --> 00:04:18,705
NEW YORK

87
00:04:18,800 --> 00:04:20,631
WEDNESDAY

88
00:04:22,596 --> 00:04:26,180
Ladies and gentlemen,
I'm the next great magician...

89
00:04:26,266 --> 00:04:28,427
...and I'll give you $100...

90
00:04:28,518 --> 00:04:30,804
...if you can tell me
how this trick is done.

91
00:04:31,062 --> 00:04:34,554
I have an ordinary spoon from Mel's Deli,
a restaurant in Brooklyn.

92
00:04:34,649 --> 00:04:36,059
Take a look.

93
00:04:36,151 --> 00:04:39,814
Now, everybody pay close attention.

94
00:04:40,030 --> 00:04:43,397
I'm about to bend the spoon
with the power of my mind.

95
00:04:53,627 --> 00:04:55,993
Thank you. Pass it around.

96
00:04:56,087 --> 00:04:57,577
- What's this?
- What's he doing?

97
00:04:57,672 --> 00:05:00,914
Looks like we've got
a spoon and a handle.

98
00:05:01,009 --> 00:05:02,124
I have other tricks.

99
00:05:02,219 --> 00:05:04,426
Just give me my $100.

100
00:05:04,513 --> 00:05:05,593
You promised.

101
00:05:05,680 --> 00:05:06,886
Nice wallet.

102
00:05:07,015 --> 00:05:08,221
You have a very good eye, sir.

103
00:05:08,308 --> 00:05:09,308
Thank you.

104
00:05:16,483 --> 00:05:17,814
The wallet.

105
00:05:18,026 --> 00:05:20,187
My wallet. My watch!

106
00:05:24,866 --> 00:05:25,946
Stop him!

107
00:05:26,034 --> 00:05:27,149
Stop the guy in the leather jacket!

108
00:05:27,244 --> 00:05:28,244
He's got my wallet!

109
00:05:28,870 --> 00:05:31,327
LOS ANGELES
THURSDAY

110
00:05:36,670 --> 00:05:38,035
All right, Los Angeles...

111
00:05:38,129 --> 00:05:39,790
...are we ready to finish this?

112
00:05:39,881 --> 00:05:40,881
Yes!

113
00:05:40,966 --> 00:05:43,002
When the countdown reaches zero...

114
00:05:43,468 --> 00:05:45,800
...a tank will be lowered...

115
00:05:45,887 --> 00:05:47,798
...full of hungry piranhas.

116
00:05:47,889 --> 00:05:50,596
Every woman needs a pair of handcuffs.
Right, ladies?

117
00:05:50,892 --> 00:05:53,554
One, two, three!

118
00:06:25,927 --> 00:06:27,713
She's serious! She can't get out!

119
00:06:30,390 --> 00:06:31,800
Get help!

120
00:06:32,183 --> 00:06:33,298
What's going on, man?

121
00:06:34,895 --> 00:06:36,851
Let me through! Move!

122
00:07:16,144 --> 00:07:18,100
Please! This is bullshit!

123
00:07:18,313 --> 00:07:20,804
Whoever invented this
is a sick sadist!

124
00:07:29,032 --> 00:07:30,442
I'm your biggest fan.

125
00:07:30,659 --> 00:07:32,445
I can tell by the way
you're attacking my face.

126
00:07:32,661 --> 00:07:33,661
Close the door.

127
00:07:33,662 --> 00:07:35,277
- Come here, magician.
- All right.

128
00:07:37,707 --> 00:07:39,117
- Wait.
- What?

129
00:07:39,334 --> 00:07:40,699
How did you do that?

130
00:07:40,919 --> 00:07:42,079
The seven of diamonds
on the tower?

131
00:07:42,295 --> 00:07:44,251
- Yeah.
- Trade secret.

132
00:07:45,966 --> 00:07:48,173
I'll give you a hint.
I had to bribe the electrician.

133
00:07:48,426 --> 00:07:49,426
How much?

134
00:07:49,552 --> 00:07:50,667
Fifty dollars.

135
00:07:50,887 --> 00:07:52,843
- It's a struggle.
- Thank you.

136
00:07:53,056 --> 00:07:54,056
Is it always the seven?

137
00:07:54,115 --> 00:07:55,505
I've got 52 versions of that trick.

138
00:07:55,725 --> 00:07:57,841
And do you have 52 tricks to perform on me?

139
00:07:58,061 --> 00:07:59,346
I'll see if I can manage.

140
00:07:59,562 --> 00:08:00,768
Magician!

141
00:08:01,815 --> 00:08:02,815
I love your shampoo.

142
00:08:04,943 --> 00:08:06,941
- Hold on.
- What?

143
00:08:08,780 --> 00:08:09,780
You have to go.

144
00:08:09,906 --> 00:08:10,906
Are you kidding me?

145
00:08:11,116 --> 00:08:13,107
Oh, my God, how embarrassing.

146
00:08:13,326 --> 00:08:16,363
Do you always do this?
Is this some twisted trick you play on girls?

147
00:08:16,579 --> 00:08:18,661
"45 East Evan Street."

148
00:08:18,873 --> 00:08:20,238
Don't worry. I'll call you.

149
00:08:20,458 --> 00:08:21,823
You don't have my number.

150
00:08:22,043 --> 00:08:23,954
I'm a magician. I'll figure it out.
Good night.

151
00:08:24,170 --> 00:08:25,660
You're an asshole.

152
00:09:38,578 --> 00:09:39,578
Hey!

153
00:09:40,413 --> 00:09:41,448
Henley.

154
00:09:41,831 --> 00:09:43,867
Danny? Hi.

155
00:09:44,375 --> 00:09:46,661
Did you get a card?

156
00:09:47,712 --> 00:09:49,828
Congratulations. Very nice.

157
00:09:50,048 --> 00:09:51,048
Here's what I'll do...

158
00:09:51,132 --> 00:09:52,338
...I'll go check out the building.

159
00:09:52,509 --> 00:09:54,545
Wait here. I'll be right back for you.
Don't go in.

160
00:09:54,761 --> 00:09:56,717
Hey, Danny!
I'm not your assistant anymore.

161
00:09:57,013 --> 00:09:58,173
Nice hair.

162
00:09:58,389 --> 00:09:59,970
What are you up to?

163
00:10:00,183 --> 00:10:02,174
You know perfectly well, Danny.

164
00:10:02,393 --> 00:10:05,055
I saw all your anonymous comments
on my website.

165
00:10:05,271 --> 00:10:08,058
You have a website?
That's great. Spread the word.

166
00:10:10,777 --> 00:10:12,108
All right.

167
00:10:12,320 --> 00:10:15,403
Apparently,
the invitation wasn't exclusive.

168
00:10:15,615 --> 00:10:18,197
That's a blow to my ego.

169
00:10:18,409 --> 00:10:19,409
Excuse me.

170
00:10:19,577 --> 00:10:21,363
- The door's locked.
- Really? Let me see.

171
00:10:21,579 --> 00:10:23,535
You. Wait. Don't say anything.

172
00:10:24,124 --> 00:10:25,660
Helen? No...

173
00:10:25,875 --> 00:10:26,875
Henley.

174
00:10:27,293 --> 00:10:28,533
It's written on your coffee.

175
00:10:28,753 --> 00:10:30,118
Thanks for ruining the trick.

176
00:10:30,338 --> 00:10:33,125
That wasn't mentalism.
It was just an observation.

177
00:10:33,341 --> 00:10:34,501
Second observation.

178
00:10:34,717 --> 00:10:37,083
You're beautiful.

179
00:10:37,303 --> 00:10:38,303
Thank you.

180
00:10:38,471 --> 00:10:40,928
Very good. Very smooth. Nice line.

181
00:10:41,057 --> 00:10:43,298
J. Daniel Atlas. Nice to meet you.

182
00:10:43,560 --> 00:10:45,175
Well, I know you,
and I want to say that...

183
00:10:45,288 --> 00:10:46,288
...I'm not interested in you using...

184
00:10:46,354 --> 00:10:47,514
...your mentalism on us.

185
00:10:47,772 --> 00:10:50,263
We don't know who summoned us
or whether this is real.

186
00:10:51,025 --> 00:10:55,109
Wait a minute, I'm sensing
you're a control freak.

187
00:10:55,196 --> 00:10:56,436
Have we met?

188
00:10:56,531 --> 00:10:57,941
You don't need to be a mentalist to see that.

189
00:10:58,032 --> 00:10:59,112
You're obsessive.

190
00:10:59,200 --> 00:11:01,282
I'll take that as a compliment.

191
00:11:01,369 --> 00:11:02,734
Only he would.

192
00:11:02,829 --> 00:11:04,820
Great. Okay. Another compliment.

193
00:11:04,914 --> 00:11:08,281
Now I see why you two broke up.

194
00:11:08,376 --> 00:11:09,456
A couple? No.

195
00:11:09,544 --> 00:11:10,750
No, we were never a couple.

196
00:11:10,837 --> 00:11:12,623
He used to saw me in half.

197
00:11:12,714 --> 00:11:13,749
She was a very good assistant.

198
00:11:13,840 --> 00:11:14,875
But too fat for Danny.

199
00:11:14,966 --> 00:11:17,799
No. I only said it once,
because of the trapdoor.

200
00:11:17,886 --> 00:11:19,877
- There were specifications.
- You made it that size.

201
00:11:19,971 --> 00:11:21,461
Nobody could fit
through that trapdoor.

202
00:11:21,556 --> 00:11:22,716
Rebecca could.

203
00:11:22,891 --> 00:11:24,472
Rebecca did, for years.

204
00:11:24,559 --> 00:11:26,220
Do you know how hard it is...

205
00:11:26,352 --> 00:11:28,183
...to walk around in those tiny outfits?

206
00:11:28,396 --> 00:11:30,227
No. I'm the star of the show.

207
00:11:30,523 --> 00:11:32,479
He never made you feel special.

208
00:11:32,567 --> 00:11:36,480
Trust me, you deserve
to feel special.

209
00:11:36,571 --> 00:11:37,981
Nice story you made up.

210
00:11:38,072 --> 00:11:39,733
You two have fun together.

211
00:11:39,991 --> 00:11:41,151
I can't believe it.

212
00:11:42,327 --> 00:11:43,908
J. Daniel Atlas?

213
00:11:44,037 --> 00:11:46,619
I've seen all your shows.

214
00:11:46,706 --> 00:11:47,991
You're like--

215
00:11:48,082 --> 00:11:49,288
You're my idol. Seriously.

216
00:11:49,417 --> 00:11:50,907
I'm a huge fan. Nice to meet you.

217
00:11:51,002 --> 00:11:52,492
My name's Jack, by the way.

218
00:11:52,587 --> 00:11:53,997
Question: Did you guys get one of these?

219
00:11:55,048 --> 00:11:56,333
Yeah. Death.

220
00:11:56,716 --> 00:11:57,922
The High Priestess.

221
00:11:58,301 --> 00:11:59,301
I'm the Lover.

222
00:11:59,385 --> 00:12:00,625
For three minutes.

223
00:12:01,471 --> 00:12:02,506
The Hermit.

224
00:12:02,722 --> 00:12:05,338
Are we expecting someone?
Why are we--

225
00:12:05,558 --> 00:12:06,889
The door's locked.

226
00:12:07,101 --> 00:12:09,638
No, anything can be opened.

227
00:12:19,322 --> 00:12:21,108
What is this place?

228
00:12:25,495 --> 00:12:27,110
And I thought
my place was filthy.

229
00:12:34,796 --> 00:12:36,957
It's freezing in here.

230
00:12:38,633 --> 00:12:39,668
What's that?

231
00:12:39,884 --> 00:12:41,090
I don't know.

232
00:12:44,347 --> 00:12:45,347
What does it say?

233
00:12:46,599 --> 00:12:48,135
"Not now."

234
00:12:48,977 --> 00:12:52,265
"A rose by any other name..."

235
00:12:57,193 --> 00:12:59,400
Guys, what's happening?

236
00:13:01,072 --> 00:13:02,562
Look at that.

237
00:13:09,330 --> 00:13:10,410
It's gas!

238
00:13:10,665 --> 00:13:12,747
Relax, it's dry ice.

239
00:13:13,543 --> 00:13:14,543
Great.

240
00:13:15,503 --> 00:13:16,993
Wait a minute. What is all this?

241
00:13:17,088 --> 00:13:18,828
Hold on.

242
00:13:23,678 --> 00:13:24,838
I'm not picking up anything.

243
00:13:25,013 --> 00:13:26,753
Thank you. Thanks for the delay.

244
00:13:26,973 --> 00:13:28,929
I'm just trying to create
an atmosphere of wisdom.

245
00:13:29,058 --> 00:13:30,594
So you're like Buddha...

246
00:13:30,685 --> 00:13:32,095
...if he weren't so enlightened.

247
00:13:32,186 --> 00:13:33,551
And you're like Jesus...

248
00:13:33,646 --> 00:13:36,183
...if he were arrogant
and all his miracles were fake.

249
00:13:36,274 --> 00:13:37,559
Lovebirds, take it somewhere private.

250
00:13:37,650 --> 00:13:39,481
Danny, tell me the truth. Was this you?

251
00:13:39,569 --> 00:13:40,684
No. Was it you?

252
00:13:40,820 --> 00:13:42,105
I wish.

253
00:13:42,613 --> 00:13:44,854
Why didn't you ask me?

254
00:13:45,867 --> 00:13:47,027
Great.

255
00:13:52,373 --> 00:13:53,863
There's no electricity.

256
00:13:55,043 --> 00:13:56,533
Let's see.

257
00:14:13,603 --> 00:14:15,059
Blueprints.

258
00:14:15,897 --> 00:14:17,262
These are incredible.

259
00:14:18,691 --> 00:14:20,022
Who could have done this?

260
00:14:20,234 --> 00:14:22,395
I don't know, but I want to meet them.

261
00:14:24,322 --> 00:14:25,903
It's a show.

262
00:14:28,368 --> 00:14:29,483
Wow.

263
00:14:42,548 --> 00:14:45,210
LAS VEGAS
ONE YEAR LATER

264
00:14:45,927 --> 00:14:47,463
Merritt McKinney.

265
00:14:48,930 --> 00:14:50,636
Daniel Atlas.

266
00:14:51,808 --> 00:14:53,469
Henley Reeves.

267
00:14:54,852 --> 00:14:56,558
Jack Wilder.

268
00:14:57,355 --> 00:15:01,314
Arthur Tressler
and the MGM Grand Hotel present...

269
00:15:04,028 --> 00:15:06,144
...the Four Horsemen.

270
00:15:07,615 --> 00:15:08,775
Thank you.

271
00:15:09,075 --> 00:15:13,785
Tonight we'd like to try something
that will set us apart from everyone else.

272
00:15:13,996 --> 00:15:15,327
Our final act...

273
00:15:15,540 --> 00:15:19,624
...will be a trick never before seen
on a Las Vegas stage.

274
00:15:20,670 --> 00:15:23,002
Or any other stage, for that matter.

275
00:15:23,798 --> 00:15:25,208
Ladies and gentlemen...

276
00:15:27,552 --> 00:15:28,667
...tonight...

277
00:15:29,554 --> 00:15:31,340
...we're going to rob a bank.

278
00:15:42,316 --> 00:15:44,398
That's a lot of enthusiasm for a crime.

279
00:15:45,153 --> 00:15:48,816
I'm excited. How about you?

280
00:15:54,287 --> 00:15:56,494
One, two, three!

281
00:16:05,256 --> 00:16:06,256
I'm sorry, sir.

282
00:16:06,340 --> 00:16:08,376
Recording the performance
is strictly forbidden.

283
00:16:08,593 --> 00:16:11,585
Of course. I'll put this away.

284
00:16:11,846 --> 00:16:13,052
Actually, you need to give it to me.

285
00:16:13,222 --> 00:16:14,962
I'll return it to you
when the show is over.

286
00:16:16,392 --> 00:16:17,392
Okay.

287
00:16:20,897 --> 00:16:22,603
All right.

288
00:16:22,857 --> 00:16:24,722
Please, calm down.

289
00:16:25,443 --> 00:16:28,276
Who has a bank
they'd like us to rob?

290
00:16:29,238 --> 00:16:31,399
Lots of people out for revenge.

291
00:16:31,616 --> 00:16:33,481
We'll choose someone at random.

292
00:16:35,077 --> 00:16:37,489
My colleagues will make sure
it's completely random.

293
00:16:38,623 --> 00:16:40,955
- Elvis, help me out.
- Thank you.

294
00:16:41,167 --> 00:16:43,704
Jack has a bowl
of balls numbered by section.

295
00:16:43,794 --> 00:16:45,785
Jack, could you give me a section number?

296
00:16:46,005 --> 00:16:47,461
Thank you. It's Section B.

297
00:16:47,548 --> 00:16:48,708
Where's Section B?

298
00:16:49,383 --> 00:16:51,874
All right. There it is.
It'll be one of you. Get ready.

299
00:16:52,386 --> 00:16:53,717
I don't know why you're all so excited.

300
00:16:53,804 --> 00:16:54,839
It'll only be them.

301
00:16:55,139 --> 00:16:56,720
Merritt, could you give me a row number?

302
00:16:57,300 --> 00:16:58,359
Thank you, Merritt.

303
00:16:58,434 --> 00:17:01,597
It's row number five.
Where is it?

304
00:17:01,812 --> 00:17:04,645
Henley, could you give me
a random seat number?

305
00:17:06,067 --> 00:17:07,102
Lucky number 13.

306
00:17:07,318 --> 00:17:08,933
B-5-13. Where are you?

307
00:17:09,153 --> 00:17:12,771
Sir, please stand up.
There you are. Hello.

308
00:17:14,408 --> 00:17:18,276
Can you confirm
that your seat is B-5-13?

309
00:17:18,277 --> 00:17:19,313
Yes.

310
00:17:19,330 --> 00:17:22,788
Great. Could you tell us your name
and the name of your bank?

311
00:17:22,959 --> 00:17:25,166
My name is Etienne Forcier.

312
00:17:25,419 --> 00:17:28,786
And my bank is
Crédit Républicain in Paris.

313
00:17:28,965 --> 00:17:30,080
French. Okay.

314
00:17:30,341 --> 00:17:31,797
We were looking for something more local.

315
00:17:31,968 --> 00:17:34,084
A credit union
with no security system.

316
00:17:34,303 --> 00:17:36,259
But that's fine. A promise is a promise.

317
00:17:36,472 --> 00:17:38,929
Please come up onstage,
and we'll rob your bank.

318
00:17:39,141 --> 00:17:40,972
While he makes his way up...

319
00:17:41,310 --> 00:17:42,470
We have someone here tonight...

320
00:17:42,687 --> 00:17:46,350
...who helped us go
from four unknown magicians...

321
00:17:46,607 --> 00:17:47,642
...struggling to--

322
00:17:47,817 --> 00:17:49,182
Well, to get here.

323
00:17:49,443 --> 00:17:50,478
You probably know him...

324
00:17:50,653 --> 00:17:53,360
...from one of the many companies
that bear his name.

325
00:17:53,614 --> 00:17:56,981
Our friend and benefactor,
Mr. Arthur Tressler.

326
00:17:57,201 --> 00:17:59,157
Please stand up, Art.

327
00:17:59,370 --> 00:18:02,157
The only man who has
the Queen's cell phone number.

328
00:18:03,124 --> 00:18:05,115
Please don't sit down, Art.

329
00:18:06,168 --> 00:18:08,284
I want to tell you that
when we approached Mr. Tressler...

330
00:18:08,504 --> 00:18:11,962
...we promised to become
the biggest name in magic.

331
00:18:12,174 --> 00:18:13,459
So we wanted to say, "Thank you."

332
00:18:13,676 --> 00:18:15,507
By the way, Art,
you'll have noticed that on the sign...

333
00:18:15,720 --> 00:18:17,802
...we made sure to put
your name at the top.

334
00:18:18,014 --> 00:18:21,973
If you're as good as you think, darling...

335
00:18:22,184 --> 00:18:24,220
...you won't have to do that
for much longer.

336
00:18:24,478 --> 00:18:25,718
We haven't gotten
to the final act yet.

337
00:18:25,980 --> 00:18:28,221
I invite you to watch it
and judge for yourself.

338
00:18:28,482 --> 00:18:30,848
Ladies and gentlemen, Arthur Tressler!

339
00:18:31,027 --> 00:18:32,312
Thank you. Once again...

340
00:18:32,528 --> 00:18:34,519
...the Cardinal of Clairvoyance,
Merritt McKinney.

341
00:18:34,739 --> 00:18:38,027
Etienne, what Jack is bringing over...

342
00:18:38,200 --> 00:18:41,818
...is what we in the magic world
call a teleportation helmet.

343
00:18:42,038 --> 00:18:44,074
You'll have to put this on,
because it will allow you...

344
00:18:44,332 --> 00:18:48,075
...to travel through time and space...

345
00:18:48,336 --> 00:18:50,497
...to the bank, in the...

346
00:18:50,713 --> 00:18:51,713
Eighth?

347
00:18:51,922 --> 00:18:53,833
- Ninth arrondissement.
- Right.

348
00:18:54,050 --> 00:18:55,711
Once you're there...

349
00:18:55,926 --> 00:18:57,882
...we'll communicate with you
through the helmet.

350
00:18:58,095 --> 00:18:59,426
Now, please...

351
00:19:00,056 --> 00:19:01,717
Oh, my God, it's beautiful.

352
00:19:01,891 --> 00:19:04,507
And very stylish, too.

353
00:19:04,727 --> 00:19:08,219
It's about time France learned
a little American style.

354
00:19:09,565 --> 00:19:11,556
- Isn't that a gorgeous accessory?
- Thank you.

355
00:19:11,901 --> 00:19:12,931
Yes.

356
00:19:13,444 --> 00:19:16,231
Thank you. Thank you very much.

357
00:19:17,406 --> 00:19:20,898
But before you go, please
pick a card. Any card.

358
00:19:21,077 --> 00:19:22,237
Not that one.

359
00:19:22,411 --> 00:19:25,744
No, that's an old joke in our country.
You can pick that one.

360
00:19:25,915 --> 00:19:27,325
All right. This card?

361
00:19:27,583 --> 00:19:30,040
Show it to your friends in Section B,
but not to us.

362
00:19:31,379 --> 00:19:33,301
Great. Now sign your name on it.

363
00:19:33,395 --> 00:19:34,396
Yes.

364
00:19:34,491 --> 00:19:35,879
In English, if possible.

365
00:19:36,258 --> 00:19:37,878
- Very good.
- Thank you.

366
00:19:38,761 --> 00:19:39,876
Put it in your pocket.

367
00:19:40,262 --> 00:19:42,628
Now there's one little detail to settle.

368
00:20:00,074 --> 00:20:04,613
Etienne, step inside
this bizarre device.

369
00:20:04,829 --> 00:20:06,740
I'm going to step back.

370
00:20:07,164 --> 00:20:09,951
Here in Las Vegas, it's 11:50 p.m.

371
00:20:10,126 --> 00:20:12,242
In Paris, it's 8:50 a.m.

372
00:20:12,461 --> 00:20:14,793
The bank opens in less than ten minutes.

373
00:20:15,005 --> 00:20:16,005
One...

374
00:20:16,132 --> 00:20:17,132
...two...

375
00:20:17,258 --> 00:20:18,293
Three.

376
00:20:21,595 --> 00:20:22,801
What the hell?

377
00:20:22,972 --> 00:20:24,587
- Wow! Etienne?
- Etienne?

378
00:20:24,807 --> 00:20:26,638
That wasn't supposed to happen, was it?

379
00:20:26,809 --> 00:20:27,809
Etienne?

380
00:20:28,477 --> 00:20:30,468
I liked the French guy.

381
00:20:30,646 --> 00:20:31,681
Where did he go?

382
00:20:32,606 --> 00:20:34,642
PARIS
AT THAT MOMENT

383
00:20:40,656 --> 00:20:42,442
Wait, here he is.

384
00:20:44,493 --> 00:20:46,529
No, please.

385
00:20:46,787 --> 00:20:48,652
This is Daniel Atlas. Can you hear me?

386
00:20:49,540 --> 00:20:52,156
- Etienne? Are you all right?
- Yes.

387
00:20:53,169 --> 00:20:54,659
What do you see there?

388
00:20:56,380 --> 00:20:57,495
Money.

389
00:20:58,007 --> 00:20:59,007
Is it real?

390
00:20:59,175 --> 00:21:01,666
Yes, it looks like three million euros.

391
00:21:03,888 --> 00:21:05,628
All right, we need you
to do the following.

392
00:21:05,848 --> 00:21:08,885
Take the card you signed
out of your pocket.

393
00:21:09,351 --> 00:21:11,512
And take out your ticket stub
from the theater as well.

394
00:21:11,729 --> 00:21:13,845
Place them right there,
in the middle of the money.

395
00:21:18,027 --> 00:21:19,642
Leave them there.

396
00:21:31,207 --> 00:21:34,324
On the side of the helmet,
you'll feel a button.

397
00:21:34,543 --> 00:21:35,703
Don't press it yet.

398
00:21:35,920 --> 00:21:37,535
That button activates
a ventilation shaft...

399
00:21:37,755 --> 00:21:40,497
...that connects Paris to Las Vegas.

400
00:21:40,716 --> 00:21:42,047
Okay. Now press it.

401
00:21:42,218 --> 00:21:44,004
Etienne, get ready.

402
00:21:44,261 --> 00:21:46,422
You may feel a vacuum.

403
00:22:17,127 --> 00:22:18,162
One second.

404
00:23:12,016 --> 00:23:14,257
Thank you, Etienne. Thank you, everyone!

405
00:23:14,351 --> 00:23:15,511
We are the Four Horsemen.

406
00:23:15,603 --> 00:23:18,390
Good night!

407
00:23:30,743 --> 00:23:31,949
Wait a minute.

408
00:23:32,328 --> 00:23:33,909
Dylan Rhodes.

409
00:23:35,414 --> 00:23:37,530
What? I must have heard you wrong.

410
00:23:37,625 --> 00:23:39,081
Did you say "magicians"?

411
00:23:39,168 --> 00:23:41,079
Yes, Dylan. Magicians.

412
00:23:41,170 --> 00:23:42,330
Where are you?

413
00:23:42,463 --> 00:23:44,419
At the Aria Hotel.
I'm on my way to arrest them.

414
00:23:44,506 --> 00:23:45,962
Aria

415
00:23:48,677 --> 00:23:50,588
FBI! Hands up!

416
00:23:50,679 --> 00:23:52,010
Come on. Up. Move.

417
00:23:53,432 --> 00:23:55,138
Come on. Hands up.

418
00:23:55,351 --> 00:23:56,636
Put that book down.

419
00:23:56,852 --> 00:23:58,592
Well, you got me.

420
00:23:58,687 --> 00:24:00,177
- Stop! Hands up!
- Jesus!

421
00:24:00,689 --> 00:24:03,431
Can somebody help us
with our luggage?

422
00:24:03,984 --> 00:24:05,019
Come on.

423
00:24:22,878 --> 00:24:24,834
Chief, tell me this is a joke.

424
00:24:24,922 --> 00:24:27,959
I just got Willy Mears
to turn on Paulie Attanasio.

425
00:24:28,050 --> 00:24:29,756
In a month...

426
00:24:29,843 --> 00:24:31,299
...I'll blow the whole thing wide open.

427
00:24:31,387 --> 00:24:32,422
Call Turkelson.

428
00:24:32,513 --> 00:24:33,628
He's in Atlantic City.

429
00:24:33,722 --> 00:24:34,928
What about Cowan?

430
00:24:35,057 --> 00:24:36,467
He's sitting there on his fat ass.

431
00:24:36,558 --> 00:24:37,764
Very funny, Rhodes.

432
00:24:38,227 --> 00:24:39,342
Love you, too.

433
00:24:39,687 --> 00:24:40,722
Asshole.

434
00:24:40,938 --> 00:24:42,474
I don't have time
for magic tricks.

435
00:24:42,564 --> 00:24:45,101
Those magic tricks cost
a French bank three million dollars.

436
00:24:45,192 --> 00:24:46,352
- That's what they stole?
- Yes.

437
00:24:46,443 --> 00:24:47,933
Actually, 3.2 million.

438
00:24:51,615 --> 00:24:52,615
Who is she?

439
00:24:53,617 --> 00:24:54,617
Who are you?

440
00:24:54,702 --> 00:24:56,363
Alma Dray, Interpol.

441
00:24:56,537 --> 00:24:57,993
I'll be investigating with you.

442
00:24:58,080 --> 00:24:59,286
You've got to be kidding me.

443
00:24:59,373 --> 00:25:00,909
It's bad enough they have me chasing...

444
00:25:01,041 --> 00:25:02,156
...David Copperfield and his gang.

445
00:25:02,251 --> 00:25:05,459
And now they saddle me with...?
Sorry. Interpol?

446
00:25:05,546 --> 00:25:07,787
I'm looking forward to working
with you too, Agent Rhodes.

447
00:25:07,881 --> 00:25:10,839
Sorry. I don't think I need
your help with this.

448
00:25:10,926 --> 00:25:13,463
- And the Frenchman?
- I've already talked to him.

449
00:25:13,554 --> 00:25:16,045
Really?
You talked to my witness before I did?

450
00:25:16,181 --> 00:25:19,298
Your witness?
I thought you didn't want the case.

451
00:25:19,393 --> 00:25:20,883
Besides, he's useless.

452
00:25:21,186 --> 00:25:22,801
Really? Why?

453
00:25:22,896 --> 00:25:25,137
Because he thinks the magic was real.

454
00:25:25,232 --> 00:25:26,893
He thinks the magic was real?

455
00:25:26,984 --> 00:25:28,440
You're right. He's useless.

456
00:25:28,527 --> 00:25:31,860
Does Interpol mind
if we talk to him?

457
00:25:31,947 --> 00:25:33,528
Thank you.

458
00:25:34,158 --> 00:25:37,321
I've never stolen anything in my life.

459
00:25:37,411 --> 00:25:39,777
When I tried to return the money,
they wouldn't take it.

460
00:25:39,872 --> 00:25:42,329
Well, let's say
you just robbed a bank.

461
00:25:42,416 --> 00:25:44,953
- I did.
- Great. Fantastic. You did.

462
00:25:45,044 --> 00:25:47,581
Since I'm new to this,
could you explain how you got...

463
00:25:47,671 --> 00:25:50,037
...from Las Vegas to Paris in three seconds?

464
00:25:50,549 --> 00:25:52,289
With the helmet.

465
00:25:52,760 --> 00:25:54,421
What the hell is this?

466
00:25:54,511 --> 00:25:56,376
They hypnotized him during the show.

467
00:25:56,472 --> 00:25:58,303
I told you he was useless.

468
00:25:58,390 --> 00:25:59,505
I'll deal with you when I'm finished...

469
00:25:59,600 --> 00:26:01,136
...with this other fairy tale.

470
00:26:04,707 --> 00:26:05,707
Hey!

471
00:26:05,773 --> 00:26:07,434
During the show,
they hypnotized the audience...

472
00:26:07,524 --> 00:26:08,764
...as if they were at the Philharmonic.

473
00:26:08,901 --> 00:26:11,187
I believe the key word was "fairy tale."

474
00:26:11,278 --> 00:26:14,736
Okay. Come on, buddy. Écoutez.
Etienne, get down from there.

475
00:26:14,823 --> 00:26:16,108
You're not at the Philharmonic.

476
00:26:16,200 --> 00:26:18,566
- How do you say "stop" in French?
- Stop.

477
00:26:18,702 --> 00:26:19,737
Tell him to stop.

478
00:26:19,828 --> 00:26:24,071
I can't.
We have to let him finish the movement.

479
00:26:24,416 --> 00:26:26,247
I need a break.

480
00:26:26,335 --> 00:26:27,825
Too many French people
in one place.

481
00:26:27,961 --> 00:26:29,451
Agent Rhodes!

482
00:26:29,546 --> 00:26:32,208
My superiors have ordered me
to provide a report.

483
00:26:32,341 --> 00:26:35,378
And until I have it,
I'm staying, whether you like it or not.

484
00:26:35,469 --> 00:26:37,050
So we can work together...

485
00:26:37,221 --> 00:26:39,086
...or you can keep repeating...

486
00:26:39,223 --> 00:26:41,339
...questions I've already asked.

487
00:26:41,475 --> 00:26:42,635
Your choice.

488
00:26:46,480 --> 00:26:47,890
I'm not finished.

489
00:26:47,981 --> 00:26:51,724
I just flew for twelve hours
after a very long day.

490
00:26:51,860 --> 00:26:54,192
And I don't handle jet lag well.

491
00:26:57,324 --> 00:26:59,690
So if you want to see who's
in the worse mood...

492
00:26:59,827 --> 00:27:01,909
...I swear you'll lose.

493
00:27:03,539 --> 00:27:05,621
Fine. Which of these idiots...

494
00:27:05,707 --> 00:27:07,117
...would you like to talk to first?

495
00:27:07,209 --> 00:27:08,244
Him?

496
00:27:13,799 --> 00:27:14,914
Dylan, for the record...

497
00:27:15,008 --> 00:27:17,124
...the effectiveness of mentalism
has never been proven.

498
00:27:17,219 --> 00:27:18,254
All right.

499
00:27:18,345 --> 00:27:19,676
It's not a science.

500
00:27:19,847 --> 00:27:21,053
It's more of an entertainment act.

501
00:27:21,181 --> 00:27:22,421
Okay.

502
00:27:22,516 --> 00:27:25,883
Just don't believe everything the guy says.
That's all.

503
00:27:26,854 --> 00:27:29,687
Please tell your colleague outside...

504
00:27:29,815 --> 00:27:31,555
...that I apologize.

505
00:27:31,650 --> 00:27:34,016
Tell him I'm sorry
about Cross-Dressing Tuesdays.

506
00:27:34,111 --> 00:27:35,271
I overstepped.

507
00:27:35,362 --> 00:27:36,568
Cross-Dressing Tuesdays?

508
00:27:36,697 --> 00:27:38,358
It's an arrangement he has with his wife.

509
00:27:38,490 --> 00:27:40,196
Or perhaps not, if you believe...

510
00:27:40,325 --> 00:27:42,737
...what Agent Fuller
says is true.

511
00:27:42,828 --> 00:27:45,410
But isn't it an FBI tradition...

512
00:27:45,539 --> 00:27:47,404
...for the men to wear dresses?

513
00:27:48,125 --> 00:27:51,834
Don't be embarrassed, Agent Fuller.

514
00:27:54,923 --> 00:27:56,504
I'm just having some fun.

515
00:27:56,592 --> 00:27:58,924
Was this your card? No.

516
00:27:59,011 --> 00:28:00,296
It's not a queen of hearts...

517
00:28:00,387 --> 00:28:01,387
...and I respect that.

518
00:28:01,471 --> 00:28:03,336
The trick works better
if I'm not handcuffed...

519
00:28:03,432 --> 00:28:04,467
...but I understand the protocol.

520
00:28:04,558 --> 00:28:05,889
All right.

521
00:28:05,976 --> 00:28:08,558
If you had nothing to do with it...

522
00:28:08,645 --> 00:28:11,227
...how did that card get into the vault?

523
00:28:11,315 --> 00:28:12,315
That would be...

524
00:28:12,399 --> 00:28:13,889
What do the kids call it?

525
00:28:14,026 --> 00:28:15,141
Yes, exactly. Magic.

526
00:28:15,235 --> 00:28:17,226
Answer the question.
Don't get smart with me.

527
00:28:17,321 --> 00:28:19,903
All right, I'm sorry.

528
00:28:20,908 --> 00:28:22,864
Keep that. Don't share it with him.

529
00:28:23,076 --> 00:28:26,068
It says here you're a mentalist.

530
00:28:26,705 --> 00:28:29,321
What's mentalism?

531
00:28:29,416 --> 00:28:32,374
A little science and a lot of tricks.

532
00:28:32,586 --> 00:28:36,420
Let's say it consists
of making educated guesses...

533
00:28:36,590 --> 00:28:39,832
...mixed with a little intuition
and the occasional...

534
00:28:41,511 --> 00:28:43,172
...voice in my head.

535
00:28:44,431 --> 00:28:47,423
It says here that you
used to be pretty famous.

536
00:28:47,517 --> 00:28:49,508
You can even hypnotize people by phone.

537
00:28:49,603 --> 00:28:52,094
You did major tours across the U.S.

538
00:28:52,189 --> 00:28:54,180
- ...and TV specials.
- The glory days.

539
00:28:54,399 --> 00:28:57,857
But your brother and manager ran off
with all the money you earned.

540
00:28:57,945 --> 00:28:59,276
You've done your homework, haven't you?

541
00:28:59,363 --> 00:29:01,194
A tax audit
for unpaid taxes.

542
00:29:01,281 --> 00:29:03,021
It took a lot of time and effort...

543
00:29:03,116 --> 00:29:05,482
...to make a comeback, didn't it?

544
00:29:05,619 --> 00:29:06,948
It certainly did.

545
00:29:06,998 --> 00:29:10,283
Thank you for that trip
down memory lane.

546
00:29:10,499 --> 00:29:12,410
As I understand it,
when the Frenchman...

547
00:29:12,542 --> 00:29:13,702
...put on the helmet--

548
00:29:13,794 --> 00:29:14,794
Listen to me.

549
00:29:14,920 --> 00:29:17,457
If you didn't rob that bank,
you knew about the robbery.

550
00:29:17,547 --> 00:29:18,662
Which makes you an accomplice.

551
00:29:18,757 --> 00:29:21,123
If you want to get out of here today,
I suggest you--

552
00:29:21,260 --> 00:29:22,295
Unless you believe...

553
00:29:22,386 --> 00:29:23,796
...there's a prosecutor in Nevada...

554
00:29:23,887 --> 00:29:25,343
...willing to explain this
to a jury...

555
00:29:25,430 --> 00:29:26,590
...we've got a show to put on.

556
00:29:26,807 --> 00:29:27,842
And you, Agent Rhodes...

557
00:29:27,933 --> 00:29:29,264
...have a case to investigate.

558
00:29:29,351 --> 00:29:30,807
Is this your first date?

559
00:29:31,895 --> 00:29:33,010
What?

560
00:29:33,105 --> 00:29:36,893
Obviously, you two barely know
each other, if at all.

561
00:29:36,984 --> 00:29:40,476
But there's palpable tension
in this room.

562
00:29:40,570 --> 00:29:42,401
And before you get involved...

563
00:29:42,489 --> 00:29:45,981
...bear in mind
that she's hiding a lot of secrets.

564
00:29:46,118 --> 00:29:47,779
And I know the first one:

565
00:29:47,869 --> 00:29:50,986
Until today, you had a desk job.
Right?

566
00:29:51,415 --> 00:29:52,871
You should have told him.

567
00:29:52,958 --> 00:29:55,119
It's awkward for him to find out this way.

568
00:29:55,210 --> 00:29:57,041
You're begging us
to arrest you, you know that?

569
00:29:57,170 --> 00:29:59,502
If you were actually going to, yes.
But you won't.

570
00:29:59,589 --> 00:30:01,045
Because if you did, it would mean...

571
00:30:01,133 --> 00:30:03,169
...that you,
the FBI and your Interpol friends...

572
00:30:03,302 --> 00:30:06,760
...actually believe,
as an institution, in magic.

573
00:30:07,848 --> 00:30:09,463
The press would have a field day.

574
00:30:09,683 --> 00:30:11,469
And we'd become
even more famous.

575
00:30:11,560 --> 00:30:12,766
And you'd look...

576
00:30:12,894 --> 00:30:14,179
...even more stupid than you already do.

577
00:30:14,271 --> 00:30:17,729
Well, not you. But he would.

578
00:30:17,858 --> 00:30:19,519
As we magicians would say, you don't have...

579
00:30:19,609 --> 00:30:21,019
..."an ace up your sleeve."

580
00:30:21,194 --> 00:30:22,274
And you know it.

581
00:30:22,362 --> 00:30:24,444
Don't win his heart just to leave him.

582
00:30:24,614 --> 00:30:27,981
Because abandonment is
a sensitive issue for him.

583
00:30:28,535 --> 00:30:29,650
Was it Mom?

584
00:30:30,370 --> 00:30:31,576
Dad.

585
00:30:31,913 --> 00:30:33,369
He has a lot of daddy issues.

586
00:30:33,457 --> 00:30:34,993
- That's enough.
- I'll tell you something...

587
00:30:35,083 --> 00:30:38,325
...an ordinary therapist would charge
$200 to $300 for this session.

588
00:30:38,462 --> 00:30:40,748
I'll settle for $10.

589
00:30:41,298 --> 00:30:44,381
So later,
if you're feeling generous...

590
00:30:44,468 --> 00:30:46,379
...I'll gladly accept a ten-dollar bill.

591
00:30:46,636 --> 00:30:48,672
Want to know
who we've interrogated?

592
00:30:48,805 --> 00:30:50,887
Mob bosses,
murderers and thieves.

593
00:30:51,099 --> 00:30:52,509
Know who put them in this chair?

594
00:30:52,601 --> 00:30:54,512
The man sitting right here.

595
00:30:54,603 --> 00:30:58,562
I'm warning you, I can keep
my determination up much longer...

596
00:30:58,648 --> 00:31:01,481
...than you can keep up
that fake arrogance.

597
00:31:01,568 --> 00:31:03,684
And the moment I see the slightest crack...

598
00:31:03,779 --> 00:31:06,441
...in that smug facade...

599
00:31:06,531 --> 00:31:07,816
...I'll be right there.

600
00:31:07,908 --> 00:31:09,489
I'll be all over you like--

601
00:31:09,576 --> 00:31:10,736
Like white on rice?

602
00:31:11,036 --> 00:31:12,526
Sorry. That was unfair.

603
00:31:12,704 --> 00:31:13,704
Let me warn you about something.

604
00:31:13,872 --> 00:31:14,907
I want you to keep coming.

605
00:31:15,123 --> 00:31:16,454
Because no matter what you think...

606
00:31:16,541 --> 00:31:19,203
...we'll always be
three steps ahead of you.

607
00:31:19,294 --> 00:31:20,500
And when you think
you're catching up...

608
00:31:20,587 --> 00:31:21,952
...we'll be right behind you.

609
00:31:22,047 --> 00:31:23,503
And the whole time, you'll be...

610
00:31:23,590 --> 00:31:24,670
...exactly where
I want you to be.

611
00:31:24,758 --> 00:31:26,373
Come closer. Get right up close...

612
00:31:26,468 --> 00:31:27,799
...because the closer you think you are...

613
00:31:27,886 --> 00:31:29,251
...the less you'll see.

614
00:31:29,346 --> 00:31:30,346
I'm going to catch you.

615
00:31:34,810 --> 00:31:37,142
Is there something wrong with that drink, miss?

616
00:31:45,445 --> 00:31:46,651
Shit!

617
00:31:47,280 --> 00:31:48,770
First rule of magic.

618
00:31:49,616 --> 00:31:51,857
Always be
the smartest person in the room.

619
00:32:14,975 --> 00:32:16,840
Boss, what do we do?
Are we letting them go?

620
00:32:17,060 --> 00:32:19,096
They practically admitted
they're going to do it again.

621
00:32:19,187 --> 00:32:21,223
Half an hour ago,
you couldn't care less about them.

622
00:32:21,314 --> 00:32:23,054
- What changed?
- I met them.

623
00:32:23,150 --> 00:32:24,150
Guys...

624
00:32:24,484 --> 00:32:26,941
...I checked out the audience.

625
00:32:27,028 --> 00:32:28,143
There were a lot of invited guests...

626
00:32:28,238 --> 00:32:29,899
...Tressler brought in
to fill the room.

627
00:32:29,990 --> 00:32:31,776
But guess who sat in the back...

628
00:32:31,867 --> 00:32:33,027
...and filmed everything.

629
00:32:33,160 --> 00:32:34,160
I don't know.

630
00:32:34,244 --> 00:32:35,700
Thaddeus Bradley.

631
00:32:38,248 --> 00:32:40,364
Thaddeus Bradley?
The TV, DVD and theater guy?

632
00:32:40,500 --> 00:32:41,500
No.

633
00:32:41,585 --> 00:32:43,917
The guy who exposes magicians.

634
00:32:44,004 --> 00:32:45,835
The one who sells them out
and reveals how they do their tricks.

635
00:32:45,922 --> 00:32:47,002
Call him.

636
00:32:47,090 --> 00:32:48,751
Already did. He's expecting you in 45 minutes.

637
00:32:48,842 --> 00:32:49,877
Good.

638
00:32:50,051 --> 00:32:52,713
Mr. Bradley started out as a magician.

639
00:32:52,804 --> 00:32:54,510
- He's pretty good.
- Really?

640
00:32:54,848 --> 00:32:56,964
- One of the best.
- You don't say.

641
00:32:58,310 --> 00:33:01,177
"The greatest genius is the one
who sees through the illusion."

642
00:33:01,271 --> 00:33:03,683
A man who loves his work.

643
00:33:05,150 --> 00:33:06,936
Sure you wouldn't like a glass of wine?

644
00:33:07,027 --> 00:33:09,860
No, thank you, sir.
We're on duty.

645
00:33:10,322 --> 00:33:11,482
I'll have one.

646
00:33:20,040 --> 00:33:22,577
Incredible. Impressive.

647
00:33:24,211 --> 00:33:25,326
Red dye in the palm.

648
00:33:25,462 --> 00:33:28,124
Is that how Jesus did it?
Amazing.

649
00:33:28,215 --> 00:33:29,546
Excuse me. Could you give me a moment?

650
00:33:29,633 --> 00:33:31,919
He was one of the greatest magicians.
Why didn't he keep performing?

651
00:33:32,552 --> 00:33:35,214
Do you know how many people
attended magic shows...

652
00:33:35,305 --> 00:33:36,886
...over the last five years?

653
00:33:36,973 --> 00:33:38,213
About 1.6 million.

654
00:33:38,308 --> 00:33:41,641
Know how many DVDs I sold
in the same period?

655
00:33:41,728 --> 00:33:43,013
Five million.

656
00:33:43,104 --> 00:33:44,139
So it's about the money.

657
00:33:44,231 --> 00:33:45,721
The money is there...

658
00:33:45,815 --> 00:33:49,057
...because the need to know
is greater than the desire to be fooled.

659
00:33:49,152 --> 00:33:50,666
Like you, I despise...

660
00:33:50,716 --> 00:33:53,441
...those who prey on
the gullibility of the masses.

661
00:33:53,532 --> 00:33:57,195
But with all due respect,
isn't there a price to pay for this game?

662
00:33:57,744 --> 00:33:58,779
A price?

663
00:33:58,870 --> 00:34:00,155
I don't know. Careers.

664
00:34:00,247 --> 00:34:01,362
Lives.

665
00:34:01,456 --> 00:34:04,664
I suppose you mean Lionel Shrike.

666
00:34:05,126 --> 00:34:06,957
Sorry, who?

667
00:34:07,045 --> 00:34:08,535
Lionel Shrike.

668
00:34:08,630 --> 00:34:12,999
A mediocre magician who drowned
in the Hudson River thirty years ago.

669
00:34:13,093 --> 00:34:14,762
In his first TV special...

670
00:34:14,812 --> 00:34:16,836
...Bradley exposed all
of Shrike's tricks.

671
00:34:16,930 --> 00:34:18,296
A year later...

672
00:34:18,346 --> 00:34:20,388
...Shrike tried to stage
a triumphant comeback.

673
00:34:20,475 --> 00:34:21,885
He locked himself inside a safe...

674
00:34:22,018 --> 00:34:23,804
...and had it dropped into the river.

675
00:34:23,895 --> 00:34:25,010
But he never surfaced.

676
00:34:25,105 --> 00:34:27,346
So he died?

677
00:34:27,440 --> 00:34:29,226
I didn't kill him.

678
00:34:29,317 --> 00:34:33,560
He killed himself trying something
he wasn't prepared to do.

679
00:34:33,655 --> 00:34:35,862
Don't you realize this is a game?

680
00:34:35,949 --> 00:34:37,735
Believe me, it's not a game.

681
00:34:37,826 --> 00:34:38,986
It is a game.

682
00:34:39,077 --> 00:34:40,817
You're the game. They're playing you.

683
00:34:40,912 --> 00:34:44,370
And your pathetic attempts
to win my trust...

684
00:34:44,457 --> 00:34:46,197
Please!

685
00:34:46,293 --> 00:34:47,293
You caught me.

686
00:34:47,377 --> 00:34:48,617
If you really want to know...

687
00:34:48,712 --> 00:34:52,500
...how they robbed a bank in Paris
from a stage in Las Vegas...

688
00:34:52,591 --> 00:34:54,547
...my special airs
next month.

689
00:34:54,634 --> 00:34:55,749
I could arrest you right now...

690
00:34:55,844 --> 00:34:58,301
...for obstructing justice
and force you to talk.

691
00:34:58,388 --> 00:34:59,924
Unless, of course, you're trying to tell me...

692
00:35:00,015 --> 00:35:03,098
...you don't actually know
how they did it.

693
00:35:05,061 --> 00:35:07,222
With showmanship and theatrics.

694
00:35:07,439 --> 00:35:09,555
When a magician waves his hands and says:

695
00:35:09,774 --> 00:35:11,355
"Here's where I'm doing the magic"...

696
00:35:11,568 --> 00:35:13,934
...the real trick
is happening somewhere else.

697
00:35:14,446 --> 00:35:17,404
Misdirection is
a basic principle of magic.

698
00:35:17,616 --> 00:35:19,902
I don't care about
the principles of magic.

699
00:35:20,076 --> 00:35:21,566
I want to know how they robbed a bank.

700
00:35:21,745 --> 00:35:24,407
If you think they robbed a bank,
you're an idiot.

701
00:35:24,956 --> 00:35:26,241
But you don't have to take my word for it.

702
00:35:26,458 --> 00:35:28,119
Why don't you go to Paris?

703
00:35:28,585 --> 00:35:32,077
Hermia? The teleportation helmet, please.

704
00:35:32,255 --> 00:35:33,586
You're kidding, right?

705
00:35:33,757 --> 00:35:35,588
Do you want to know how they did it or not?

706
00:35:36,760 --> 00:35:38,751
I'm going to put this on your head.

707
00:35:40,138 --> 00:35:41,469
Step up.

708
00:35:42,724 --> 00:35:44,055
There you go.

709
00:35:45,101 --> 00:35:48,719
Now let's tighten it up.

710
00:35:49,773 --> 00:35:52,435
Make yourself comfortable. Here we go.

711
00:35:52,651 --> 00:35:55,393
When you're ready to travel to Paris...

712
00:35:55,612 --> 00:35:57,398
...say the magic word.

713
00:35:57,614 --> 00:35:58,774
Suck my dick?

714
00:35:59,574 --> 00:36:00,609
That'll do.

715
00:36:13,296 --> 00:36:16,459
I prefer to take the stairs
to get to Paris.

716
00:36:16,925 --> 00:36:18,836
Welcome to the City of Light.

717
00:36:19,094 --> 00:36:21,130
Very funny. What was the helmet for?

718
00:36:21,346 --> 00:36:22,461
To amuse myself.

719
00:36:24,182 --> 00:36:25,182
What?

720
00:36:25,266 --> 00:36:26,346
Nothing.

721
00:36:28,478 --> 00:36:29,513
So they have a vault.

722
00:36:29,771 --> 00:36:30,771
Yes.

723
00:36:30,814 --> 00:36:33,021
And it's an exact replica
of that Frenchman's bank.

724
00:36:33,274 --> 00:36:34,274
The Frenchman was a plant.

725
00:36:34,359 --> 00:36:35,769
The Frenchman was tricked.

726
00:36:35,985 --> 00:36:38,317
They picked him.
They knew which seat he was in.

727
00:36:38,488 --> 00:36:40,979
No, the audience picked the seat.

728
00:36:41,449 --> 00:36:44,156
Except they didn't.

729
00:36:44,369 --> 00:36:46,405
They made the audience think
they were choosing it.

730
00:36:46,663 --> 00:36:48,028
We'll choose someone at random.

731
00:36:48,289 --> 00:36:51,998
In fact, they had hidden the ball
with the seat number they wanted.

732
00:36:52,502 --> 00:36:54,538
It's row number five.

733
00:36:54,796 --> 00:36:57,162
Can you give me a random seat number?
Lucky number 13.

734
00:36:57,465 --> 00:36:59,126
Can you confirm
that's your seat?

735
00:36:59,342 --> 00:37:00,878
They had chosen him.

736
00:37:01,678 --> 00:37:03,589
They programmed his mind
to go to Las Vegas.

737
00:37:03,680 --> 00:37:04,680
Viva Las Vegas.

738
00:37:06,015 --> 00:37:09,052
And they reinforced that idea
until he went.

739
00:37:10,311 --> 00:37:11,517
They followed him.

740
00:37:11,730 --> 00:37:12,845
They studied him.

741
00:37:13,064 --> 00:37:14,804
Nothing was left to chance.

742
00:37:16,985 --> 00:37:18,191
I've got the measurements.

743
00:37:18,361 --> 00:37:20,693
He had no idea he was the chosen one.

744
00:37:20,989 --> 00:37:22,525
And in Vegas, they triggered him.

745
00:37:22,741 --> 00:37:23,981
When I snap my fingers...

746
00:37:24,200 --> 00:37:26,657
Once again, Merritt McKinney.

747
00:37:26,870 --> 00:37:28,076
And how did they know which bank it was?

748
00:37:28,329 --> 00:37:29,329
You're kidding.

749
00:37:29,330 --> 00:37:32,663
Seriously? Could you be
a little more condescending?

750
00:37:32,876 --> 00:37:33,876
Yes.

751
00:37:34,252 --> 00:37:35,332
Like this.

752
00:37:36,337 --> 00:37:39,545
There's a strange and original method...

753
00:37:39,716 --> 00:37:43,174
...magicians use to figure out
which banks their audience uses.

754
00:37:44,220 --> 00:37:45,335
Are you ready?

755
00:37:45,555 --> 00:37:46,840
Yes.

756
00:37:47,390 --> 00:37:48,755
A credit card.

757
00:37:49,225 --> 00:37:52,012
That's also how they got
the signature on the card...

758
00:37:52,228 --> 00:37:54,219
...they left in the Paris vault.

759
00:37:54,439 --> 00:37:55,439
Very good.

760
00:37:55,565 --> 00:37:56,896
They beat you to it, Agent.

761
00:37:57,108 --> 00:38:00,396
But they still had to get
the card inside the vault.

762
00:38:00,570 --> 00:38:01,685
According to you,
they didn't steal the money.

763
00:38:01,946 --> 00:38:05,029
I didn't say they didn't take the money.
I said they didn't rob the bank.

764
00:38:06,034 --> 00:38:09,902
My guess is they targeted
a shipment of brand-new bills...

765
00:38:10,079 --> 00:38:11,489
...headed for the Frenchman's bank...

766
00:38:11,748 --> 00:38:13,579
...in an armored truck...

767
00:38:15,293 --> 00:38:18,080
...which is also very hard to access...

768
00:38:18,296 --> 00:38:20,912
...unless you're already inside.

769
00:38:40,610 --> 00:38:43,647
And despite what banks
would have us believe...

770
00:38:43,738 --> 00:38:46,525
...the guys driving those trucks
are weak-minded.

771
00:38:48,284 --> 00:38:49,820
For the Four Horsemen...

772
00:38:49,911 --> 00:38:52,152
...it was almost too easy.

773
00:38:58,461 --> 00:38:59,621
Hello.

774
00:39:06,010 --> 00:39:09,594
How did they make the fake money
disappear from the vault? What's that?

775
00:39:10,014 --> 00:39:11,424
Flash paper.

776
00:39:13,685 --> 00:39:15,892
Magicians use it all the time.

777
00:39:21,067 --> 00:39:23,854
It leaves no smoke or residue.

778
00:39:29,242 --> 00:39:31,574
Two days ago, nobody knew them...

779
00:39:31,786 --> 00:39:34,368
...but tonight's tickets
sold out in 35 seconds.

780
00:39:34,455 --> 00:39:36,912
I think they've cracked
the secret formula for fame.

781
00:39:37,000 --> 00:39:39,742
Give away three million dollars
to the audience.

782
00:39:39,961 --> 00:39:41,292
Then they leave happy, right?

783
00:39:41,421 --> 00:39:42,627
The ultimate closing act.

784
00:39:42,714 --> 00:39:45,171
They'll be the world's first magicians
to actually get laid.

785
00:39:45,258 --> 00:39:47,715
Remember,
if the oxygen mask drops...

786
00:39:47,802 --> 00:39:49,838
- ...put it on the lawyer first.
- Yeah.

787
00:39:49,929 --> 00:39:52,261
I always do that. Lawyer first...

788
00:39:52,348 --> 00:39:54,384
- ...then me, and then the kids.
- Then you.

789
00:39:54,475 --> 00:39:55,885
Yes. Then the kids.

790
00:39:56,769 --> 00:39:59,431
Let me put into words
what we're both thinking.

791
00:39:59,522 --> 00:40:00,682
What's that?

792
00:40:00,773 --> 00:40:03,856
Obviously, you have feelings for Daniel.

793
00:40:03,943 --> 00:40:06,104
They're unrequited and misplaced...

794
00:40:06,195 --> 00:40:08,982
...because he's emotionally unavailable.

795
00:40:09,073 --> 00:40:11,735
So you're very...

796
00:40:11,826 --> 00:40:13,441
How shall I put it?

797
00:40:13,953 --> 00:40:14,953
Bottled up.

798
00:40:15,038 --> 00:40:16,038
Really?

799
00:40:16,122 --> 00:40:19,740
Recognizing that you have
unresolved physical needs...

800
00:40:19,834 --> 00:40:22,450
...and with the sole intention
of helping a friend...

801
00:40:22,545 --> 00:40:27,209
...I invite you to imagine
I could be your corkscrew.

802
00:40:27,467 --> 00:40:28,582
Wow. Thank you.

803
00:40:28,676 --> 00:40:31,418
I'll consider
that cheap, tacky offer of sex.

804
00:40:31,512 --> 00:40:35,425
It may be cheap and tacky, but
it won't take up much of your time.

805
00:40:39,062 --> 00:40:41,053
We'll be arriving a few minutes early.

806
00:40:41,147 --> 00:40:42,227
Sit back and relax.

807
00:40:42,315 --> 00:40:44,055
We'll soon be in New Orleans.

808
00:40:44,150 --> 00:40:45,856
Thank you for flying with us.

809
00:40:45,944 --> 00:40:48,401
What does Tressler get out of it besides money?

810
00:40:48,488 --> 00:40:49,523
Ego.

811
00:40:51,240 --> 00:40:52,980
Pick a card.

812
00:40:53,493 --> 00:40:55,950
Look at it and put it back in the deck.
Then tell me--

813
00:40:56,037 --> 00:40:57,868
No, don't tell me.

814
00:40:57,956 --> 00:40:59,742
- All right.
- Okay.

815
00:40:59,832 --> 00:41:01,242
Thaddeus was there that night.

816
00:41:01,334 --> 00:41:03,746
He knows exactly how they did it.
What about him?

817
00:41:03,836 --> 00:41:05,497
What are you suggesting?

818
00:41:05,588 --> 00:41:07,078
You think he might be in on it.

819
00:41:07,173 --> 00:41:08,583
I don't know. I just know these guys...

820
00:41:08,675 --> 00:41:10,131
...were a bunch of street magicians...

821
00:41:10,218 --> 00:41:11,754
...a year ago, with zero resources.

822
00:41:11,844 --> 00:41:14,381
How do they go from that to this...

823
00:41:14,472 --> 00:41:15,928
...without outside help?

824
00:41:16,015 --> 00:41:17,425
Was this your card?

825
00:41:17,517 --> 00:41:20,884
No, my card is over there,
on that man's lap.

826
00:41:21,562 --> 00:41:23,268
Well shuffled.

827
00:41:23,356 --> 00:41:24,516
This isn't easy.

828
00:41:24,607 --> 00:41:25,642
According to this book...

829
00:41:25,733 --> 00:41:29,772
...some people practice a single move
eight hours a day for years.

830
00:41:29,862 --> 00:41:32,899
Really? Some people need
to get a life.

831
00:41:32,991 --> 00:41:35,232
Do you have a problem
with magicians in general?

832
00:41:35,326 --> 00:41:37,533
Or just these ones?

833
00:41:38,287 --> 00:41:40,744
I don't care about magicians in general.

834
00:41:40,957 --> 00:41:43,744
I just hate people
who take advantage of others.

835
00:41:43,960 --> 00:41:44,960
How do they take advantage of them?

836
00:41:45,086 --> 00:41:46,086
Here we go again.

837
00:41:47,547 --> 00:41:49,378
They exploit their weaknesses...

838
00:41:49,590 --> 00:41:52,172
...their need to believe in something
inexplicable...

839
00:41:52,427 --> 00:41:55,260
...to make life more bearable.

840
00:41:55,471 --> 00:41:59,589
I see it as a strength.
My life is happier when I believe in it.

841
00:42:00,226 --> 00:42:01,557
Was this your card?

842
00:42:02,770 --> 00:42:03,885
Yes.

843
00:42:04,772 --> 00:42:05,772
Yes?

844
00:42:05,898 --> 00:42:07,889
- Yes.
- All right.

845
00:42:09,902 --> 00:42:12,609
Is that smile real?

846
00:42:13,281 --> 00:42:14,316
Maybe.

847
00:42:14,991 --> 00:42:18,779
Tell me something, Detective.
Do you feel taken advantage of?

848
00:42:18,995 --> 00:42:22,237
Or did you have even a little fun?

849
00:42:24,500 --> 00:42:25,615
A guy goes to the doctor.

850
00:42:25,835 --> 00:42:28,121
The doctor says,
"You'll have to stop masturbating."

851
00:42:28,296 --> 00:42:30,503
"Why?"
"So I can finish examining you."

852
00:42:30,757 --> 00:42:33,294
Danny, can we talk
about my role in the show?

853
00:42:33,509 --> 00:42:34,509
Hey...

854
00:42:35,261 --> 00:42:37,092
...we need to get ready
for the show.

855
00:42:38,181 --> 00:42:39,181
Now?

856
00:42:39,348 --> 00:42:41,339
Don't do that.
You're not doing that to me.

857
00:42:41,601 --> 00:42:42,807
What? I'm just looking at you.

858
00:42:42,977 --> 00:42:44,467
No. I've been watching you for a year.

859
00:42:44,645 --> 00:42:45,851
I know all your tricks.

860
00:42:46,105 --> 00:42:47,140
That's what you call them? Tricks?

861
00:42:47,356 --> 00:42:48,971
They're gimmicks.
You use the Barnum effect.

862
00:42:49,150 --> 00:42:50,515
You read people's eyes
and body language.

863
00:42:50,777 --> 00:42:52,608
If it's so easy,
why don't you do it to her?

864
00:42:52,820 --> 00:42:54,310
Yeah, Danny, why don't you do it to me?

865
00:42:54,530 --> 00:42:56,486
No, you're too easy.
I'd rather do Jasmine.

866
00:42:56,783 --> 00:42:57,783
No.

867
00:42:58,618 --> 00:42:59,618
Me.

868
00:42:59,702 --> 00:43:00,987
Yes, Art.

869
00:43:01,204 --> 00:43:02,204
All right.

870
00:43:02,330 --> 00:43:03,365
Better.

871
00:43:03,452 --> 00:43:04,453
- Art.
- Yes.

872
00:43:04,540 --> 00:43:07,282
I warn you, if I want to,
I'm hard to read.

873
00:43:07,502 --> 00:43:09,458
Focus on this, okay?

874
00:43:10,338 --> 00:43:12,124
As a kid, you were tough.

875
00:43:12,340 --> 00:43:14,171
A real little rascal.

876
00:43:14,467 --> 00:43:16,298
You had a dog that was really fierce.

877
00:43:16,511 --> 00:43:18,217
An aggressive breed, a--

878
00:43:18,471 --> 00:43:20,507
A bulldog named Ben.

879
00:43:20,723 --> 00:43:22,964
Actually, I was a wimp as a kid.

880
00:43:23,226 --> 00:43:26,684
And I had a fluffy kitten
named Snuffles.

881
00:43:28,281 --> 00:43:29,340
I'm sorry.

882
00:43:29,357 --> 00:43:31,518
Let me try. I can do better.

883
00:43:31,692 --> 00:43:32,692
Another chance.

884
00:43:32,860 --> 00:43:34,350
Jack can do better.

885
00:43:34,529 --> 00:43:35,529
Let's try family.

886
00:43:35,738 --> 00:43:38,730
You had an uncle on your mother's side.

887
00:43:38,991 --> 00:43:41,482
He had a...
A really masculine name.

888
00:43:41,702 --> 00:43:43,738
He was a down-to-earth guy...

889
00:43:43,996 --> 00:43:45,827
Who didn't take any nonsense-- Like--

890
00:43:46,040 --> 00:43:49,373
Was his name Paul... Thompson?
Was it Paul...?

891
00:43:50,211 --> 00:43:51,326
You know what? I've got nothing.

892
00:43:51,587 --> 00:43:52,587
You were close.

893
00:43:52,588 --> 00:43:56,172
- Really?
- My uncle's name was Cushman Armitage.

894
00:43:56,551 --> 00:43:59,088
Really? Snuffles and Cushman Armitage?
That was your childhood?

895
00:43:59,345 --> 00:44:01,711
I hope tonight's show
goes better than this.

896
00:44:02,765 --> 00:44:04,721
Don't worry. You'll see.

897
00:44:10,189 --> 00:44:13,181
Give me some good news
about a hotel room.

898
00:44:13,401 --> 00:44:14,401
Are you serious?

899
00:44:14,569 --> 00:44:18,027
No. It's Mardi Gras.
There's nothing near the theater.

900
00:44:18,239 --> 00:44:20,446
Hold on. Where did that woman go?

901
00:44:22,451 --> 00:44:24,407
Hello! There you are.

902
00:44:24,704 --> 00:44:27,070
Is the Frenchwoman out sightseeing
now that she's in New Orleans?

903
00:44:27,290 --> 00:44:30,908
No, actually,
speaking French here is an advantage.

904
00:44:31,419 --> 00:44:33,205
See that woman over there?

905
00:44:33,921 --> 00:44:34,921
That's Marie Claire.

906
00:44:35,131 --> 00:44:37,417
And she has an apartment
that's perfect for us.

907
00:44:39,719 --> 00:44:40,754
You're welcome.

908
00:44:41,429 --> 00:44:42,429
Yes.

909
00:44:42,763 --> 00:44:44,094
We've got the place.

910
00:44:46,392 --> 00:44:49,600
I want two inside here.
The other thirty around the perimeter.

911
00:44:49,687 --> 00:44:51,052
Rooftops, alleys and balconies.

912
00:44:51,147 --> 00:44:54,230
- Keep eyes on the Horsemen at all times.
- Yes, sir.

913
00:44:54,317 --> 00:44:58,151
You know, a hundred years ago, William Robinson
took the name Chung Ling Soo...

914
00:44:58,237 --> 00:45:02,105
...and spent his whole life
never speaking English in public.

915
00:45:03,951 --> 00:45:05,782
The other guy, Maximillian--

916
00:45:05,995 --> 00:45:08,953
I don't care about the history of magic.

917
00:45:09,373 --> 00:45:11,534
If you want to keep playing
their game, go ahead.

918
00:45:11,751 --> 00:45:13,742
I'm just trying to understand how they think.

919
00:45:13,920 --> 00:45:16,036
You think I'm playing their game?

920
00:45:16,255 --> 00:45:19,042
I don't know how you expect
all that to work against this.

921
00:45:19,258 --> 00:45:20,748
Against what? Magic?

922
00:45:22,762 --> 00:45:24,093
Lionel Shrike.

923
00:45:24,263 --> 00:45:27,630
In Central Park,
he asks a guy to sign a card.

924
00:45:27,892 --> 00:45:30,850
Then he walks up to a tree
that's been there twenty years.

925
00:45:31,103 --> 00:45:32,309
They cut the tree in half.

926
00:45:32,563 --> 00:45:35,248
Inside the trunk,
in a glass box...

927
00:45:35,298 --> 00:45:36,431
...is the signed card.

928
00:45:36,651 --> 00:45:37,731
How did he do it?

929
00:45:37,985 --> 00:45:41,102
I have no idea,
but there has to be a logical explanation.

930
00:45:44,450 --> 00:45:45,906
Do we have an agent at the entrance yet?

931
00:45:46,118 --> 00:45:47,483
No, but we've got good news.

932
00:45:47,745 --> 00:45:49,610
Remember how Atlas is
a control freak...

933
00:45:49,789 --> 00:45:51,450
...and his crew
uses electronic bracelets?

934
00:45:51,624 --> 00:45:52,739
Electronic bracelets?

935
00:45:53,000 --> 00:45:55,787
Everyone on his crew wears
electronic tracking bracelets.

936
00:45:56,003 --> 00:45:58,210
They're calibrated to a sub-eight flat band.

937
00:45:58,464 --> 00:46:00,250
- As long as Atlas tracks them...
- Next.

938
00:46:00,466 --> 00:46:03,082
...we can track him
from our mobile unit.

939
00:46:03,302 --> 00:46:05,133
If you detect movement, call me.

940
00:46:05,304 --> 00:46:06,510
Copy that.

941
00:46:15,481 --> 00:46:17,767
You're missing the bigger picture.

942
00:46:19,151 --> 00:46:22,769
In Paris, there's a place
called the Pont des Arts...

943
00:46:22,989 --> 00:46:26,777
...where I sit some mornings.

944
00:46:27,201 --> 00:46:30,864
People come, make a wish,
and hang it there on a padlock.

945
00:46:31,122 --> 00:46:33,784
They throw the key into the Seine.

946
00:46:35,626 --> 00:46:40,165
People do it all the time:
mothers, couples, elderly people.

947
00:46:40,464 --> 00:46:43,126
When they see the key fall into the water...

948
00:46:43,676 --> 00:46:46,133
...they know their secret
is locked away forever.

949
00:46:46,679 --> 00:46:51,139
It's real and magical at the same time.

950
00:46:57,189 --> 00:46:58,304
So...

951
00:46:59,191 --> 00:47:02,854
Do you really believe
there might be a fifth Horseman?

952
00:47:04,322 --> 00:47:05,322
Yes.

953
00:47:07,700 --> 00:47:10,863
MAGIC EXPOSED
WITH THADDEUS BRADLEY

954
00:47:12,038 --> 00:47:15,872
Legend has it that a secret order
arose in Egypt called...

955
00:47:16,375 --> 00:47:17,375
..."The Eye."

956
00:47:19,337 --> 00:47:21,703
They say its members
perfected sleight of hand...

957
00:47:21,881 --> 00:47:25,840
...to steal food from the pharaohs
and give it to the slaves.

958
00:47:26,344 --> 00:47:27,344
To what end?

959
00:47:27,553 --> 00:47:31,546
To use magic and illusion
to balance the scales of justice.

960
00:47:32,391 --> 00:47:34,399
Could the Horsemen be the latest example...

961
00:47:34,449 --> 00:47:36,725
...in the long tradition
of gullible people who believe that?

962
00:47:37,104 --> 00:47:41,723
Will they defy the laws of law and
logic as they did in Las Vegas?

963
00:47:42,693 --> 00:47:46,936
Or will the dark mystique
of the Southern swamps...

964
00:47:47,531 --> 00:47:48,941
...finally thwart them?

965
00:47:55,581 --> 00:47:57,037
Bravo, Mr. Bradley.

966
00:47:57,249 --> 00:48:01,583
Unless you have a restraining order,
which you don't, get out of here.

967
00:48:01,796 --> 00:48:05,584
If I want something restrained...

968
00:48:06,759 --> 00:48:07,919
...I'll restrain it.

969
00:48:08,094 --> 00:48:09,254
Take a break.

970
00:48:09,595 --> 00:48:11,426
According to
your potential investors...

971
00:48:11,597 --> 00:48:15,215
...you could make
a maximum of 2.5 million...

972
00:48:15,434 --> 00:48:16,924
...from your television special.

973
00:48:17,144 --> 00:48:19,806
I'm offering you a check...

974
00:48:21,607 --> 00:48:22,767
...for 3.5 million.

975
00:48:23,067 --> 00:48:24,067
To make me go away?

976
00:48:24,235 --> 00:48:27,944
To make you fly away.
My plane is at your disposal.

977
00:48:30,074 --> 00:48:32,736
Let's not kid ourselves, Mr. Tressler.

978
00:48:34,328 --> 00:48:36,444
You win if they win.

979
00:48:36,956 --> 00:48:38,992
I win if they lose.

980
00:48:39,250 --> 00:48:42,287
I take it we don't have a deal.

981
00:48:43,462 --> 00:48:47,796
And therein lies the conflict.

982
00:48:51,512 --> 00:48:52,797
I'll be direct.

983
00:48:53,180 --> 00:48:57,640
My bank account is
considerably larger than yours.

984
00:48:57,852 --> 00:49:00,810
And my lawyers are far more powerful.

985
00:49:01,021 --> 00:49:04,479
They'll tie you up...

986
00:49:05,359 --> 00:49:07,270
...with tons of injunctions...

987
00:49:07,486 --> 00:49:09,602
...until you're helplessly watching...

988
00:49:09,822 --> 00:49:12,484
...everything you own
go down the drain...

989
00:49:13,325 --> 00:49:15,657
...before those greedy eyes of yours.

990
00:49:15,953 --> 00:49:18,160
It's not the first time I've been threatened.

991
00:49:19,540 --> 00:49:21,030
And I'm still here.

992
00:49:21,125 --> 00:49:25,459
But it is the first time
you've been threatened by me.

993
00:49:26,338 --> 00:49:30,752
And if you do anything
to tarnish my investment...

994
00:49:30,843 --> 00:49:33,209
...that loss you just mentioned...

995
00:49:33,304 --> 00:49:38,549
...will be swift, steep
and measurable on the Richter scale.

996
00:49:40,186 --> 00:49:42,177
Wow. You shouldn't have done that.

997
00:49:43,522 --> 00:49:45,103
According to superstition...

998
00:49:45,316 --> 00:49:48,308
...anyone who uses a doll
to vent their anger...

999
00:49:48,402 --> 00:49:52,862
...is likely to have that anger
turned back on them.

1000
00:49:53,699 --> 00:49:55,189
Isn't that curious?

1001
00:49:56,035 --> 00:49:58,026
I thought you didn't believe in magic.

1002
00:50:07,963 --> 00:50:09,043
Do me a favor.

1003
00:50:09,131 --> 00:50:13,170
Picture your most daring
sexual experience.

1004
00:50:13,260 --> 00:50:14,295
Stay out of my head.

1005
00:50:14,386 --> 00:50:18,049
No, if I can't get inside your head,
I'll never get between your legs.

1006
00:50:18,182 --> 00:50:20,173
- Sure.
- Let's try this exercise.

1007
00:50:20,267 --> 00:50:22,132
- And it helps...
- Sorry to interrupt.

1008
00:50:22,228 --> 00:50:24,810
I wanted to wish you
good luck tonight.

1009
00:50:24,897 --> 00:50:27,479
So you can expose us later
on your website?

1010
00:50:27,566 --> 00:50:28,726
And whenever the public wants to see.

1011
00:50:28,859 --> 00:50:31,817
- That's not going to happen.
- No?

1012
00:50:31,904 --> 00:50:34,896
Are you operating from a special plane
for the Eye?

1013
00:50:35,616 --> 00:50:40,451
I hear it's a magical place.
Lots of stardust and moonlight.

1014
00:50:40,538 --> 00:50:42,995
It makes every magician's dreams come true.

1015
00:50:43,082 --> 00:50:44,492
You've come a long way...

1016
00:50:44,583 --> 00:50:46,414
...far beyond
what anyone expected...

1017
00:50:46,502 --> 00:50:48,493
...for a bunch of ambitious has-beens.

1018
00:50:48,587 --> 00:50:52,751
If by "has-been" you mean me...

1019
00:50:52,841 --> 00:50:54,573
...let me tell you
I'm flattered...

1020
00:50:54,623 --> 00:50:56,208
...because I've always
thought of myself as a nobody.

1021
00:50:56,303 --> 00:50:58,464
May I give you
a quick reading?

1022
00:50:59,265 --> 00:51:00,265
By all means.

1023
00:51:01,267 --> 00:51:05,886
I see a little boy.

1024
00:51:06,021 --> 00:51:08,933
He wants to be a great magician someday.

1025
00:51:09,024 --> 00:51:10,764
And although he's good,
he's not great.

1026
00:51:10,901 --> 00:51:14,985
So he ends up at the bottom
of the entertainment food chain...

1027
00:51:15,072 --> 00:51:18,610
...feeding off those with
the talent he never had.

1028
00:51:18,742 --> 00:51:21,279
Tell me. Did I get anything right?

1029
00:51:23,956 --> 00:51:26,948
Wait. Before you go. I'm working
on something new. Can you help?

1030
00:51:27,626 --> 00:51:30,459
All right. Name a card.

1031
00:51:30,546 --> 00:51:32,127
- King of hearts.
- I knew it.

1032
00:51:32,214 --> 00:51:33,420
All right.

1033
00:51:40,639 --> 00:51:43,722
- It's in your left sleeve.
- Is it?

1034
00:51:43,809 --> 00:51:45,549
No. I don't see it.

1035
00:51:45,644 --> 00:51:49,353
Why don't you check
that unnecessary corduroy pocket?

1036
00:51:51,191 --> 00:51:52,191
Yeah.

1037
00:51:53,861 --> 00:51:55,567
TAKE THIS

1038
00:51:55,654 --> 00:51:58,284
Thanks for stopping by,
but this is...

1039
00:51:58,334 --> 00:52:00,614
...a restricted area
for talented people, so...

1040
00:52:01,910 --> 00:52:02,910
Good luck.

1041
00:52:02,995 --> 00:52:05,156
And to you, not so much.

1042
00:52:05,247 --> 00:52:06,532
Ladies and gentlemen...

1043
00:52:06,624 --> 00:52:08,285
...the Savoy welcomes you...

1044
00:52:08,417 --> 00:52:10,078
...to tonight's special performance.

1045
00:52:10,169 --> 00:52:12,911
The Four Horsemen, Act Two.

1046
00:52:13,005 --> 00:52:15,041
Unlike
traditional magicians...

1047
00:52:15,132 --> 00:52:17,293
...the Four Horsemen
invite you to film...

1048
00:52:17,384 --> 00:52:18,384
...call your friends...

1049
00:52:18,469 --> 00:52:21,552
...post your videos online
or stream them live.

1050
00:52:21,639 --> 00:52:25,006
Thank you. The show will start
in a few minutes.

1051
00:52:28,979 --> 00:52:30,469
Nice watch.

1052
00:52:31,649 --> 00:52:33,981
It's a family heirloom.

1053
00:52:37,196 --> 00:52:39,903
By the way, I know how he did the trick.

1054
00:52:39,990 --> 00:52:41,571
- Who?
- Shrike.

1055
00:52:41,659 --> 00:52:43,900
When he was fourteen...

1056
00:52:43,994 --> 00:52:46,155
...he saw a hole
in a tree in Central Park.

1057
00:52:46,246 --> 00:52:49,909
He asked a guy
who worked at the carousel...

1058
00:52:50,000 --> 00:52:52,616
...to sign a card
for an ordinary trick.

1059
00:52:52,711 --> 00:52:53,871
And?

1060
00:52:53,962 --> 00:52:58,126
At the guy's retirement party,
eighteen years later...

1061
00:52:58,217 --> 00:53:01,459
...Shrike showed up,
asked him to sign a card...

1062
00:53:01,553 --> 00:53:03,134
...and voilà!

1063
00:53:03,222 --> 00:53:04,962
The card was already in the tree.

1064
00:53:05,057 --> 00:53:06,922
It spent eighteen years in the tree.

1065
00:53:07,017 --> 00:53:08,473
The card was there.

1066
00:53:08,560 --> 00:53:11,115
Over eighteen years, the trunk
grew around it.

1067
00:53:11,165 --> 00:53:12,553
That's not magic. It isn't.

1068
00:53:12,648 --> 00:53:15,560
The point is
you shouldn't look too closely.

1069
00:53:15,651 --> 00:53:17,607
You have to look from far enough away...

1070
00:53:17,736 --> 00:53:20,523
...to see
what happened twenty years ago.

1071
00:53:20,614 --> 00:53:23,481
When Shrike drowned,
nobody ever found the body.

1072
00:53:23,575 --> 00:53:25,361
What are you suggesting?

1073
00:53:28,997 --> 00:53:31,158
Arthur Tressler presents...

1074
00:53:31,250 --> 00:53:32,365
...Jack Wilder...

1075
00:53:32,501 --> 00:53:33,832
...Henley Reeves...

1076
00:53:33,919 --> 00:53:34,954
...Merritt McKinney...

1077
00:53:35,045 --> 00:53:36,706
...and Daniel Atlas.

1078
00:53:36,797 --> 00:53:38,753
The Four Horsemen.

1079
00:53:43,929 --> 00:53:45,590
Thank you. Before we begin...

1080
00:53:45,681 --> 00:53:47,717
...we'd like to acknowledge
the presence of two people.

1081
00:53:47,808 --> 00:53:50,265
A man and a woman
to whom we dedicate tonight's show.

1082
00:53:50,352 --> 00:53:52,183
FBI Agent Dylan Rhodes...

1083
00:53:52,271 --> 00:53:55,438
...and the lovely,
though somewhat inexperienced...

1084
00:53:55,488 --> 00:53:57,982
...Interpol agent Alma Dray.

1085
00:53:58,902 --> 00:54:03,020
Yes, Agent Rhodes has personally promised
to catch us.

1086
00:54:03,115 --> 00:54:07,575
And we invite him to try,
if he has the skill and the courage.

1087
00:54:11,874 --> 00:54:14,035
What is magic?

1088
00:54:14,126 --> 00:54:16,617
In our opinion,
it's nothing but a carefully planned deception.

1089
00:54:18,255 --> 00:54:19,620
I want you to watch.

1090
00:54:20,257 --> 00:54:22,213
Watch closely, as closely as possible.

1091
00:54:22,718 --> 00:54:26,552
Because the tricks you're about to see
may appear unconnected.

1092
00:54:26,764 --> 00:54:28,629
But we assure you
there is a connection.

1093
00:54:29,600 --> 00:54:32,433
Will it be a hundred different tricks?

1094
00:54:32,770 --> 00:54:36,479
Or one enormous illusion?

1095
00:54:44,114 --> 00:54:46,947
Now you'll see
one of the most classic tricks.

1096
00:54:47,159 --> 00:54:49,150
Danny, if you don't mind,
open the box...

1097
00:54:49,411 --> 00:54:51,447
...and show them
it's completely empty.

1098
00:54:51,830 --> 00:54:55,823
We'll need twelve brave volunteers.

1099
00:54:56,084 --> 00:54:58,166
I'll take little Fluffy...

1100
00:54:58,420 --> 00:55:01,082
...and place her
inside the mystery box.

1101
00:55:01,632 --> 00:55:04,590
Among you are two pencils.
Hold them up so we can see who has them.

1102
00:55:04,802 --> 00:55:06,133
- Here!
- Yes!

1103
00:55:06,678 --> 00:55:08,509
Now you're going to say the magic word.

1104
00:55:08,764 --> 00:55:09,844
Abracadabra.

1105
00:55:10,098 --> 00:55:12,965
And I'll wave the magic wand,
just because. And then...

1106
00:55:13,185 --> 00:55:15,642
Fluffy disappears
right before your eyes.

1107
00:55:17,022 --> 00:55:19,263
If you've never experienced
mass hypnosis...

1108
00:55:19,358 --> 00:55:20,518
...you're about to.

1109
00:55:20,776 --> 00:55:21,856
Sleep.

1110
00:55:21,944 --> 00:55:24,777
Down. Sleep. Completely asleep.

1111
00:55:24,863 --> 00:55:26,444
Good. Sleep.

1112
00:55:26,532 --> 00:55:28,568
Tonight we're going to shatter...

1113
00:55:28,659 --> 00:55:30,775
...some of the myths of magic.

1114
00:55:30,869 --> 00:55:34,032
We all know Fluffy
is alive and well.

1115
00:55:34,331 --> 00:55:36,868
And so Fluffy miraculously reappears.

1116
00:55:36,959 --> 00:55:38,449
And here's the mirror...

1117
00:55:38,544 --> 00:55:40,956
...that makes the box
appear empty.

1118
00:55:41,046 --> 00:55:43,128
When I say "freeze"...

1119
00:55:43,340 --> 00:55:45,547
...you'll all be
American football players...

1120
00:55:46,051 --> 00:55:48,542
...and you'll have to tackle...

1121
00:55:48,720 --> 00:55:50,051
...tear apart...

1122
00:55:50,222 --> 00:55:51,382
...crush...

1123
00:55:51,640 --> 00:55:53,926
...and destroy...

1124
00:55:54,351 --> 00:55:56,057
...the quarterback.

1125
00:55:57,437 --> 00:56:00,929
We all know the trick
of pulling a rabbit out of a hat.

1126
00:56:01,024 --> 00:56:04,016
But has anyone ever seen
a hat pulled out of a rabbit?

1127
00:56:08,448 --> 00:56:10,905
You'll know who he is.

1128
00:56:10,993 --> 00:56:14,326
He's the one who says the word.

1129
00:56:14,413 --> 00:56:15,573
Freeze.

1130
00:56:26,383 --> 00:56:28,374
Three, two, one!

1131
00:56:35,726 --> 00:56:37,557
Hands in the middle. One, two, three...

1132
00:56:37,728 --> 00:56:39,059
Let's kill the quarterback!

1133
00:56:39,229 --> 00:56:40,890
Return to your seats.

1134
00:56:41,064 --> 00:56:43,055
I have a feeling you'll be back soon.

1135
00:57:15,641 --> 00:57:17,427
Looks like you've lost weight.

1136
00:57:32,824 --> 00:57:33,984
During intermission...

1137
00:57:34,242 --> 00:57:36,528
...we asked you to write down
your account balances...

1138
00:57:36,787 --> 00:57:37,993
...and seal the paper in an envelope.

1139
00:57:38,246 --> 00:57:41,079
Now take out those envelopes.
Everybody, take them out.

1140
00:57:42,459 --> 00:57:43,824
And now I want...

1141
00:57:44,086 --> 00:57:46,998
...you to shout your names.
All at once. Now.

1142
00:57:48,757 --> 00:57:49,757
Shout your names.

1143
00:57:52,344 --> 00:57:54,300
Clement? Frannick?

1144
00:57:54,471 --> 00:57:55,471
Yes! Here!

1145
00:57:55,639 --> 00:57:56,639
Way up there.

1146
00:57:56,640 --> 00:57:58,596
All right. Dina? Robertson?

1147
00:57:58,809 --> 00:57:59,809
That's me!

1148
00:58:00,268 --> 00:58:02,600
All right. Names. Come on.

1149
00:58:03,981 --> 00:58:05,937
Josepha? Hickey?

1150
00:58:06,149 --> 00:58:07,149
That's me!

1151
00:58:07,150 --> 00:58:10,017
Josepha, focus on your balance...

1152
00:58:10,278 --> 00:58:12,189
...and count out loud from one to ten.

1153
00:58:12,447 --> 00:58:15,029
- One, two, three, four, five...
- Stop.

1154
00:58:15,534 --> 00:58:17,024
Is the first digit five?

1155
00:58:17,369 --> 00:58:18,369
Yes.

1156
00:58:18,453 --> 00:58:19,659
Do it again, faster.

1157
00:58:19,830 --> 00:58:21,286
One, two, three, four, five, six--

1158
00:58:21,498 --> 00:58:22,583
Stop.

1159
00:58:22,874 --> 00:58:24,489
Six. Again.

1160
00:58:24,710 --> 00:58:25,710
One, two--

1161
00:58:25,961 --> 00:58:29,874
Josepha, is your current account balance
$562?

1162
00:58:30,132 --> 00:58:32,293
Yes. That's it.

1163
00:58:34,011 --> 00:58:35,547
Unfortunately, you're wrong.

1164
00:58:35,804 --> 00:58:36,839
All right, Dina.

1165
00:58:38,682 --> 00:58:39,717
One...

1166
00:58:40,350 --> 00:58:41,350
...four...

1167
00:58:42,060 --> 00:58:43,060
...seven...

1168
00:58:44,021 --> 00:58:45,021
- ...seven.
- Yes.

1169
00:58:45,564 --> 00:58:49,352
That's what you think, but actually, you're wrong.

1170
00:58:49,568 --> 00:58:50,603
Clement.

1171
00:58:50,861 --> 00:58:53,819
You don't have $6,500
in your account.

1172
00:58:54,031 --> 00:58:57,819
In fact... I want you to stand up.
Everybody stand.

1173
00:58:59,628 --> 00:59:00,687
Yes.

1174
00:59:00,704 --> 00:59:03,036
Hold your envelopes against your foreheads.

1175
00:59:04,082 --> 00:59:05,538
Concentrate on the numbers.

1176
00:59:09,838 --> 00:59:12,875
This is... Wow, just what I feared.

1177
00:59:13,717 --> 00:59:15,207
This is strange.

1178
00:59:15,594 --> 00:59:18,757
I'm sorry to tell you
you're all wrong.

1179
00:59:19,014 --> 00:59:20,879
Every single one of you
is mistaken...

1180
00:59:21,058 --> 00:59:22,889
...about your bank account balances.

1181
00:59:23,101 --> 00:59:24,841
You can sit down now.

1182
00:59:25,228 --> 00:59:27,389
Oh, I almost forgot.

1183
00:59:27,564 --> 00:59:30,351
This show wouldn't be possible...

1184
00:59:30,567 --> 00:59:34,606
...if not for our generous
benefactor, Arthur Tressler.

1185
00:59:35,572 --> 00:59:37,233
A big round of applause!

1186
00:59:37,407 --> 00:59:39,898
Why don't you come onstage
for the final act?

1187
00:59:40,077 --> 00:59:41,738
- Come on, Art!
- Come on, Art!

1188
00:59:41,953 --> 00:59:43,739
There he comes. All right.

1189
00:59:43,955 --> 00:59:44,955
Are we on alert?

1190
00:59:45,082 --> 00:59:47,038
Red alert. Everyone ready.

1191
00:59:47,250 --> 00:59:48,581
Nobody leaves the theater. Nobody.

1192
00:59:48,752 --> 00:59:49,787
There he comes.

1193
00:59:50,420 --> 00:59:52,456
Art, did you fill out your envelope?

1194
00:59:52,672 --> 00:59:54,958
You didn't need to. We did it for you.

1195
00:59:55,175 --> 00:59:58,042
Art, I took a risk.

1196
00:59:58,178 --> 01:00:00,385
More than 140, right?

1197
01:00:01,431 --> 01:00:04,093
I mean 140 million, of course.

1198
01:00:07,687 --> 01:00:08,687
I'm sorry, Merritt.

1199
01:00:08,855 --> 01:00:11,938
How can he be right
when everyone else is wrong?

1200
01:00:12,150 --> 01:00:15,517
Well, maybe he's wrong too.

1201
01:00:15,737 --> 01:00:17,819
Take out your papers.

1202
01:00:17,989 --> 01:00:20,526
Use the flashlight
under your seat...

1203
01:00:20,742 --> 01:00:22,482
...to heat up the paper.

1204
01:00:22,744 --> 01:00:26,236
I think the correct balance
will start to appear.

1205
01:00:29,626 --> 01:00:32,834
Art, don't worry,
we have a flashlight for you.

1206
01:00:33,046 --> 01:00:34,081
Yes.

1207
01:00:40,846 --> 01:00:41,846
Look.

1208
01:00:42,189 --> 01:00:43,248
Wow.

1209
01:00:43,265 --> 01:00:44,471
What's going on, Daniel?

1210
01:00:44,599 --> 01:00:46,135
That's strange. A second ago it said...

1211
01:00:46,268 --> 01:00:50,181
...$144,579,651.

1212
01:00:50,313 --> 01:00:51,313
But now...

1213
01:00:51,648 --> 01:00:54,640
There's $70,000 less.

1214
01:00:55,777 --> 01:00:56,777
$562

1215
01:00:56,836 --> 01:00:58,021
$70,562

1216
01:00:58,238 --> 01:01:00,274
- Josepha, could you stand up?
- Yes.

1217
01:01:00,949 --> 01:01:02,530
What's your new balance?

1218
01:01:02,784 --> 01:01:07,118
There's $70,562 in my account.

1219
01:01:10,375 --> 01:01:13,742
Is it possible that Josepha's balance
increased...

1220
01:01:13,962 --> 01:01:16,044
...by the same amount Art's decreased?

1221
01:01:16,256 --> 01:01:18,747
Look. It's happening again.

1222
01:01:18,967 --> 01:01:19,967
Isn't it?

1223
01:01:21,261 --> 01:01:23,718
Wow, yes.

1224
01:01:23,930 --> 01:01:27,093
Art's balance went down another $280,000.

1225
01:01:27,309 --> 01:01:29,345
Dina Robertson? How about yours?

1226
01:01:29,561 --> 01:01:33,349
$281,477.

1227
01:01:37,319 --> 01:01:38,980
We have a confession to make.

1228
01:01:39,196 --> 01:01:41,482
She's right. We lied about one thing.

1229
01:01:41,698 --> 01:01:43,063
We didn't choose you at random.

1230
01:01:43,283 --> 01:01:45,740
You all have one thing in common.

1231
01:01:45,952 --> 01:01:47,817
You were all victims...

1232
01:01:48,038 --> 01:01:50,996
...of the disaster that struck one
of our most cherished cities.

1233
01:01:51,208 --> 01:01:53,745
Some lost their homes, their cars.

1234
01:01:53,960 --> 01:01:55,951
- Their businesses.
- Their loved ones.

1235
01:01:56,171 --> 01:01:59,254
But you were all insured
by the same company.

1236
01:01:59,507 --> 01:02:01,168
Tressler Insurance.

1237
01:02:02,636 --> 01:02:03,636
And they abandoned you.

1238
01:02:03,845 --> 01:02:06,382
- They tricked you with the fine print...
- ...of the policy.

1239
01:02:07,515 --> 01:02:10,131
This is all an act, right?

1240
01:02:10,352 --> 01:02:12,809
Because we're doing it on a stage?

1241
01:02:13,021 --> 01:02:14,636
Then yes, it's an act.

1242
01:02:14,856 --> 01:02:17,347
I've got $82,000 in my account!

1243
01:02:17,567 --> 01:02:19,558
That's what my phone says!

1244
01:02:19,778 --> 01:02:22,941
Check your phones!

1245
01:02:30,622 --> 01:02:31,622
Is this for real?

1246
01:02:31,831 --> 01:02:33,037
I don't know.

1247
01:02:33,250 --> 01:02:34,365
Is this really happening?

1248
01:02:34,584 --> 01:02:36,120
I don't know! I can't figure it out!

1249
01:02:36,920 --> 01:02:39,127
Did you do this?

1250
01:02:40,257 --> 01:02:41,463
How could we, Art?

1251
01:02:41,675 --> 01:02:42,835
We don't have your password.

1252
01:02:43,051 --> 01:02:46,293
We'd need access to information
that's unavailable to us.

1253
01:02:46,513 --> 01:02:48,469
Like your security questions.
For example...

1254
01:02:48,682 --> 01:02:51,890
...your mother's maiden name
or your first pet's name.

1255
01:02:52,143 --> 01:02:54,134
How would we get that information?

1256
01:02:54,354 --> 01:02:56,015
You wouldn't tell us anything.

1257
01:02:58,900 --> 01:03:01,016
It's confirmed. It's real. They robbed him.

1258
01:03:03,029 --> 01:03:04,485
Don't let them escape.

1259
01:03:04,698 --> 01:03:06,780
We left you the plane and the Rolls-Royce.

1260
01:03:13,623 --> 01:03:14,954
You'll pay for this!

1261
01:03:17,877 --> 01:03:18,877
Stop!

1262
01:03:19,629 --> 01:03:21,415
Nobody move!

1263
01:03:23,341 --> 01:03:24,456
Freeze!

1264
01:03:24,676 --> 01:03:26,166
The quarterback!

1265
01:03:29,139 --> 01:03:31,130
We are the Four Horsemen.

1266
01:03:31,641 --> 01:03:32,676
Good night.

1267
01:03:44,946 --> 01:03:46,277
Get them off me!

1268
01:03:47,032 --> 01:03:48,738
Get out!

1269
01:03:59,210 --> 01:04:00,575
Let me go!

1270
01:04:09,012 --> 01:04:11,173
Everyone outside! Everybody!

1271
01:04:17,937 --> 01:04:19,893
- Quarterback!
- Quarterback!

1272
01:04:21,358 --> 01:04:22,848
I'll meet you at the entrance.

1273
01:04:22,942 --> 01:04:24,523
And bring the tracker.

1274
01:04:24,611 --> 01:04:26,977
Come on! Now!

1275
01:04:27,072 --> 01:04:28,187
Give me the tracker.

1276
01:04:28,656 --> 01:04:29,656
Let's go.

1277
01:04:33,787 --> 01:04:35,072
FBI! Move!

1278
01:04:35,789 --> 01:04:36,949
- Dylan!
- Damn it.

1279
01:04:37,040 --> 01:04:38,040
What have you got for me?

1280
01:04:38,124 --> 01:04:39,614
That's him. Follow the red dot.

1281
01:04:39,709 --> 01:04:42,746
Give me that. Come on.
Box them in. Cut them off.

1282
01:04:44,714 --> 01:04:46,204
He turned left on Burgundy.

1283
01:05:22,961 --> 01:05:25,919
Move!

1284
01:05:28,591 --> 01:05:30,297
He's heading north on Bourbon.

1285
01:05:30,385 --> 01:05:32,250
Excuse me. Move!

1286
01:05:32,345 --> 01:05:34,336
Excuse me. Move!

1287
01:05:45,817 --> 01:05:47,478
Lost him on Bourbon.

1288
01:05:52,866 --> 01:05:54,857
Damn it! Where are you?

1289
01:06:06,588 --> 01:06:07,794
Excuse me.

1290
01:06:14,095 --> 01:06:16,051
He's heading south on Exchange Place.

1291
01:06:18,266 --> 01:06:19,346
Stop!

1292
01:06:26,524 --> 01:06:27,730
What are you doing?

1293
01:06:31,279 --> 01:06:32,940
He went into Napoleon House.

1294
01:06:35,408 --> 01:06:37,023
Move!

1295
01:06:37,785 --> 01:06:39,321
Get out of the way!

1296
01:06:41,873 --> 01:06:42,873
Move!

1297
01:06:45,126 --> 01:06:47,412
He's in the bathroom.

1298
01:06:47,754 --> 01:06:49,460
No!

1299
01:06:51,674 --> 01:06:52,880
Freeze!

1300
01:06:53,885 --> 01:06:55,091
Lower your weapons!

1301
01:06:55,303 --> 01:06:56,303
What?

1302
01:06:56,596 --> 01:06:58,587
I'm tracking myself!

1303
01:06:59,098 --> 01:07:02,682
The Horsemen made a mockery
of law enforcement agencies.

1304
01:07:02,894 --> 01:07:06,432
Their act called into question
the effectiveness of the FBI...

1305
01:07:06,648 --> 01:07:10,857
...and Special Agent Dylan Rhodes,
who's heading the investigation...

1306
01:07:11,069 --> 01:07:16,029
...who was publicly humiliated
and even tackled during tonight's show.

1307
01:07:16,241 --> 01:07:17,356
He completely lost control.

1308
01:07:17,575 --> 01:07:19,440
The story went national...

1309
01:07:19,702 --> 01:07:22,614
...and the Horsemen pulled off
the impossible...

1310
01:07:22,830 --> 01:07:24,821
...for the second time in two days.

1311
01:07:25,041 --> 01:07:28,204
Last night in Las Vegas,
the Horsemen robbed a bank in Paris...

1312
01:07:28,419 --> 01:07:29,419
Hello.

1313
01:07:31,047 --> 01:07:32,047
What are you doing?

1314
01:07:32,048 --> 01:07:33,413
Having a drink.

1315
01:07:36,553 --> 01:07:37,918
I came looking for you.

1316
01:07:38,346 --> 01:07:39,552
I'm worried about you.

1317
01:07:39,764 --> 01:07:41,300
I'm worried about you.

1318
01:07:41,516 --> 01:07:42,596
You had him.

1319
01:07:42,809 --> 01:07:44,970
He wasn't armed.
I couldn't shoot him, and you know it.

1320
01:07:45,228 --> 01:07:48,061
Yeah, but you let him go.

1321
01:07:48,523 --> 01:07:49,729
Didn't you?

1322
01:07:50,525 --> 01:07:53,062
A little French girl appears out of nowhere,
pretends to be my partner...

1323
01:07:53,278 --> 01:07:55,269
- ...then lets the bad guy escape.
- Let go of me.

1324
01:07:55,488 --> 01:07:57,194
Who are you?

1325
01:07:59,158 --> 01:08:01,240
Weren't you a desk agent?

1326
01:08:05,999 --> 01:08:07,205
Not bad.

1327
01:08:09,210 --> 01:08:10,210
What's this?

1328
01:08:13,298 --> 01:08:14,708
What's the Eye of Horus?

1329
01:08:14,924 --> 01:08:17,916
Honestly,
that's not something I want to discuss today.

1330
01:08:18,136 --> 01:08:20,673
You're condescending enough
when you're sober.

1331
01:08:20,930 --> 01:08:22,841
Get back on your own.

1332
01:08:27,103 --> 01:08:30,561
Mr. Bradley? Mr. Tressler
invites you for a drink.

1333
01:08:41,034 --> 01:08:42,034
Thank you.

1334
01:08:43,578 --> 01:08:45,785
What's your role with them?

1335
01:08:45,997 --> 01:08:46,997
My role?

1336
01:08:47,206 --> 01:08:48,787
You know everything about them.

1337
01:08:49,000 --> 01:08:50,740
What they'll do, where they'll do it.

1338
01:08:50,960 --> 01:08:54,043
If it makes you feel better,
it wasn't personal.

1339
01:08:54,255 --> 01:08:57,998
Explain to me how
it wasn't personal.

1340
01:08:58,259 --> 01:09:00,875
It's a magic trick, Mr. Tressler.

1341
01:09:01,095 --> 01:09:03,427
A magic trick on a global scale.

1342
01:09:03,806 --> 01:09:07,765
And you, sir, are the abracadabra.

1343
01:09:08,353 --> 01:09:11,095
You're the distraction
while they set up the real trick.

1344
01:09:11,314 --> 01:09:15,774
A $140 million
distraction?

1345
01:09:16,361 --> 01:09:17,521
Yes.

1346
01:09:18,196 --> 01:09:22,565
That very ego that drove you
to get involved with them...

1347
01:09:22,784 --> 01:09:24,240
...is what's stopping you from seeing the truth.

1348
01:09:24,452 --> 01:09:27,285
- You know what?
- You can destroy me, yes. I know.

1349
01:09:27,830 --> 01:09:29,491
But you won't.

1350
01:09:29,624 --> 01:09:31,785
And you won't destroy them either.

1351
01:09:31,918 --> 01:09:34,034
I don't know what that grand trick is...

1352
01:09:34,253 --> 01:09:37,040
...but it was designed years ago.

1353
01:09:37,256 --> 01:09:41,795
And I think what's coming
will be truly astonishing.

1354
01:09:42,637 --> 01:09:47,176
I suggest you relax
and enjoy your front-row seat.

1355
01:09:47,642 --> 01:09:50,304
You paid a lot of money for it.

1356
01:09:50,520 --> 01:09:52,556
I don't know how much you expect to make...

1357
01:09:54,691 --> 01:09:58,229
...but I'll double it
if you expose them now.

1358
01:09:58,444 --> 01:10:00,025
And destroy them.

1359
01:10:00,238 --> 01:10:02,229
I expect to make five million.

1360
01:10:02,949 --> 01:10:04,405
Do I look hesitant?

1361
01:10:06,327 --> 01:10:07,908
No.

1362
01:10:51,289 --> 01:10:52,620
THE GUARDIANS OF HORUS

1363
01:11:28,868 --> 01:11:29,983
Hey.

1364
01:11:31,370 --> 01:11:33,577
I'm sorry about your arm.

1365
01:11:33,664 --> 01:11:35,245
No. I'm sorry.

1366
01:11:35,333 --> 01:11:37,574
I was drunk and I mistreated you.

1367
01:11:39,545 --> 01:11:42,161
I'm very confused.

1368
01:11:44,091 --> 01:11:49,461
When Merritt said I hardly ever left
my desk, he was right.

1369
01:11:49,555 --> 01:11:52,843
I'm a researcher.
It's what I do best.

1370
01:11:53,976 --> 01:11:57,844
What's all this about?

1371
01:11:58,564 --> 01:12:01,226
Apparently, if you believe it...

1372
01:12:01,317 --> 01:12:02,682
..."The Eye" are the guardians...

1373
01:12:02,777 --> 01:12:03,937
...of real magic...

1374
01:12:04,028 --> 01:12:06,940
...and protectors
of those who practice it.

1375
01:12:07,031 --> 01:12:08,942
"Candidates for initiation...

1376
01:12:09,033 --> 01:12:12,196
...must follow a set of
commandments with blind obedience."

1377
01:12:13,204 --> 01:12:16,412
- This isn't real.
- But some people believe it is.

1378
01:12:16,874 --> 01:12:18,364
May I?

1379
01:12:20,211 --> 01:12:24,500
According to this, they only accept
new members twice a century.

1380
01:12:24,590 --> 01:12:28,003
What? Are the Horsemen putting on
these shows to get in?

1381
01:12:28,094 --> 01:12:29,300
- I don't know.
- I don't know.

1382
01:12:29,387 --> 01:12:34,381
I suppose these guys
could just rob banks for the money.

1383
01:12:34,475 --> 01:12:36,761
They wouldn't need to put on a show.

1384
01:12:36,894 --> 01:12:38,384
Maybe it's just a myth.

1385
01:12:38,479 --> 01:12:42,597
But none of it makes sense, and I don't think
logic will help us solve it.

1386
01:12:42,692 --> 01:12:45,274
- You don't say?
- No.

1387
01:12:46,529 --> 01:12:51,148
I think some things
can only be discovered...

1388
01:12:51,242 --> 01:12:55,611
...if you take certain liberties.

1389
01:13:02,628 --> 01:13:05,745
- I've got to...
- Yeah.

1390
01:13:05,840 --> 01:13:08,001
- Thanks. Good night.
- Yeah, good night.

1391
01:13:08,092 --> 01:13:09,878
- I'm sorry.
- It's okay.

1392
01:13:12,388 --> 01:13:15,846
If the Eye existed, we'd have to
assume they're watching us, right?

1393
01:13:15,933 --> 01:13:17,173
Shit.

1394
01:13:17,268 --> 01:13:19,850
I think somebody's watching us,
but it isn't magic.

1395
01:13:19,979 --> 01:13:22,937
Your fears are justified.
This is not, and never was, your phone.

1396
01:13:23,024 --> 01:13:27,734
It's an exact clone,
except for this.

1397
01:13:27,820 --> 01:13:29,105
A microphone.

1398
01:13:29,196 --> 01:13:33,030
Someone listened to all the calls
and read every text.

1399
01:13:33,284 --> 01:13:34,490
That's how they stayed ahead of us.

1400
01:13:34,619 --> 01:13:35,704
Damn it!

1401
01:13:36,787 --> 01:13:38,573
I want the video
of Atlas's interrogation.

1402
01:13:38,789 --> 01:13:40,325
Yes, sir. It's on the hard drive.

1403
01:13:42,168 --> 01:13:43,203
There it is.

1404
01:13:43,711 --> 01:13:45,451
All right, fast-forward.

1405
01:13:45,838 --> 01:13:47,248
Further. Stop!

1406
01:13:48,090 --> 01:13:49,876
Frame by frame. Come on.

1407
01:13:50,760 --> 01:13:51,795
Now normal speed.

1408
01:13:53,387 --> 01:13:55,343
Stop. Zoom in.

1409
01:13:55,681 --> 01:13:57,467
There it is. That's when he took my phone.

1410
01:13:58,768 --> 01:13:59,883
Is this thing transmitting?

1411
01:14:00,102 --> 01:14:01,512
Only when he calls or sends a text.

1412
01:14:01,729 --> 01:14:02,729
And I've already removed
the microphone.

1413
01:14:02,813 --> 01:14:04,349
Put it back
and turn on the phone.

1414
01:14:04,565 --> 01:14:06,351
They don't know we found the microphone.

1415
01:14:06,567 --> 01:14:09,934
Now we're ahead of them.
We have to keep that advantage.

1416
01:14:10,054 --> 01:14:11,093
Understood?

1417
01:14:11,113 --> 01:14:12,478
Find my phone and track it.

1418
01:14:12,698 --> 01:14:15,940
Already did.
Twenty minutes ago, it was in New York.

1419
01:14:16,160 --> 01:14:17,195
Good.

1420
01:14:17,578 --> 01:14:20,445
I want to cut them off at the source.
Have they got power? Cut it.

1421
01:14:20,665 --> 01:14:23,953
Phones, electricity, water?
Shut it all off.

1422
01:14:24,168 --> 01:14:26,910
I want to crush them.
Make sure they feel our presence.

1423
01:14:27,129 --> 01:14:28,585
And I want a plane!

1424
01:14:31,384 --> 01:14:33,170
The firewall's down.
Do they know?

1425
01:14:33,227 --> 01:14:34,286
Who?

1426
01:14:34,345 --> 01:14:36,085
"They," the people who brought us together.

1427
01:14:36,347 --> 01:14:37,507
Who brought us together?

1428
01:14:37,640 --> 01:14:38,640
Do we want to go to prison for them?

1429
01:14:38,724 --> 01:14:39,724
DEATH

1430
01:14:39,809 --> 01:14:40,809
Don't be paranoid.

1431
01:14:41,018 --> 01:14:42,098
It could happen.

1432
01:14:42,311 --> 01:14:43,801
It happened to you.
It won't happen to all of us.

1433
01:14:44,021 --> 01:14:46,854
I don't know if I can go on.
I don't want to go to prison.

1434
01:14:47,066 --> 01:14:48,146
Then don't screw up.

1435
01:14:48,359 --> 01:14:49,849
If you want to be treated like an adult...

1436
01:14:50,069 --> 01:14:51,354
...act like one.

1437
01:14:51,570 --> 01:14:53,606
Stick to the plan.
Stay here and burn everything.

1438
01:15:06,752 --> 01:15:07,958
I don't know what I'm doing here.

1439
01:15:08,170 --> 01:15:09,170
I don't know what you'd do
anywhere else.

1440
01:15:09,380 --> 01:15:11,792
I want the treasure
at the end of the rainbow.

1441
01:15:12,008 --> 01:15:14,124
That's all. Then I'm out.

1442
01:15:14,343 --> 01:15:16,379
Do whatever you want
when we're done...

1443
01:15:16,595 --> 01:15:18,586
...but until then,
stick to the plan.

1444
01:15:18,806 --> 01:15:20,842
Guys, they're here.

1445
01:15:22,685 --> 01:15:23,685
My men are ready.

1446
01:15:23,711 --> 01:15:24,750
Let's make this quick--

1447
01:15:24,770 --> 01:15:26,101
- No.
- What?

1448
01:15:26,313 --> 01:15:27,393
She has no
jurisdiction here.

1449
01:15:27,606 --> 01:15:28,721
She's Interpol. She's with me.

1450
01:15:28,941 --> 01:15:29,941
She's staying.

1451
01:15:29,942 --> 01:15:31,182
What?

1452
01:15:31,402 --> 01:15:32,402
Stay in the car.

1453
01:15:34,655 --> 01:15:35,861
Let's go.

1454
01:15:41,662 --> 01:15:42,697
All right. Let's do it.

1455
01:15:47,001 --> 01:15:48,086
FBI!

1456
01:15:52,256 --> 01:15:53,587
Ladies first.

1457
01:15:54,341 --> 01:15:55,371
Don't move!

1458
01:16:01,390 --> 01:16:04,723
Tell me,
are you spending my money wisely?

1459
01:16:05,227 --> 01:16:07,092
Extremely wisely.

1460
01:16:08,898 --> 01:16:10,388
Now let me ask you.

1461
01:16:11,192 --> 01:16:12,853
How do you want the Horsemen?

1462
01:16:13,069 --> 01:16:15,105
Chopped or fried?

1463
01:16:15,321 --> 01:16:16,321
In little pieces.

1464
01:16:17,448 --> 01:16:18,858
I'll tell the chef.

1465
01:17:34,483 --> 01:17:35,483
Stop!

1466
01:17:42,700 --> 01:17:43,815
Dylan!

1467
01:17:44,577 --> 01:17:45,577
Wait a minute!

1468
01:17:53,669 --> 01:17:54,669
Come here!

1469
01:17:56,422 --> 01:17:58,333
The fifth floor's clear.
Awaiting instructions.

1470
01:17:58,674 --> 01:17:59,674
You son of a bitch!

1471
01:17:59,717 --> 01:18:00,717
Idiot!

1472
01:18:00,759 --> 01:18:01,759
What game are you playing?

1473
01:18:01,802 --> 01:18:02,917
What game are you playing?

1474
01:18:03,429 --> 01:18:05,340
The sixth floor's clear. Move to seven.

1475
01:18:05,556 --> 01:18:07,717
Copy. Seventh floor. Let's go!

1476
01:18:08,392 --> 01:18:10,383
No, no! Come back!

1477
01:18:13,856 --> 01:18:15,062
Come here!

1478
01:18:46,096 --> 01:18:47,211
Come here!

1479
01:18:47,306 --> 01:18:48,967
Wait a minute!

1480
01:18:50,935 --> 01:18:52,766
- Seriously?
- Yes.

1481
01:19:01,779 --> 01:19:03,235
You goddamn idiot...

1482
01:19:19,296 --> 01:19:20,296
Shit!

1483
01:19:38,732 --> 01:19:39,767
Shit!

1484
01:19:41,151 --> 01:19:42,516
Give me that!

1485
01:20:08,512 --> 01:20:10,468
All units to the north exit!

1486
01:20:10,556 --> 01:20:11,841
There's nobody on Evan.

1487
01:20:11,932 --> 01:20:13,763
Block all the exits.

1488
01:20:13,851 --> 01:20:15,341
Come back!

1489
01:20:16,854 --> 01:20:18,275
FBI. Or something like that.
I need your car. Thanks.

1490
01:20:18,325 --> 01:20:19,415
What?

1491
01:20:22,067 --> 01:20:23,648
Stop!

1492
01:20:39,835 --> 01:20:40,835
Hey!

1493
01:21:10,778 --> 01:21:11,779
All right. Follow him!

1494
01:21:11,867 --> 01:21:13,903
Never tell me
to stay in the car again!

1495
01:21:13,994 --> 01:21:15,325
I wasn't in command of tactical.

1496
01:21:15,412 --> 01:21:17,778
If you don't trust me, just say so.

1497
01:21:17,873 --> 01:21:19,454
Promise you'll back me up
next time.

1498
01:21:19,541 --> 01:21:21,577
Can we talk about this later,
please?

1499
01:21:21,668 --> 01:21:23,249
No!

1500
01:21:23,337 --> 01:21:25,578
All right, I promise. Drive!

1501
01:21:33,055 --> 01:21:35,546
- Where did they go?
- Where are they?

1502
01:21:35,641 --> 01:21:37,302
I don't know. She took some guy's car.

1503
01:21:37,393 --> 01:21:38,849
- Where are they?
- Over there.

1504
01:21:38,936 --> 01:21:39,936
- Is that your car?
- Yes.

1505
01:21:40,062 --> 01:21:41,518
Let's go.

1506
01:22:07,131 --> 01:22:08,131
There he is!

1507
01:22:20,018 --> 01:22:21,018
Move aside!

1508
01:22:25,399 --> 01:22:26,434
To the left!

1509
01:22:28,777 --> 01:22:30,563
We're heading east on FDR Drive.

1510
01:22:30,654 --> 01:22:32,485
- We're not going east.
- North!

1511
01:22:32,573 --> 01:22:34,609
- We're heading north on FDR Drive.
- What?

1512
01:22:34,700 --> 01:22:36,782
We're reporting live from the FDR...

1513
01:22:36,869 --> 01:22:39,781
...where the New York police
and the FBI, as you can see...

1514
01:22:39,872 --> 01:22:41,783
...are chasing
a black sedan...

1515
01:22:41,874 --> 01:22:43,114
...at high speed.

1516
01:22:48,297 --> 01:22:49,297
Fuller, where are you?

1517
01:22:49,548 --> 01:22:51,334
Right here. Trying to catch up to you.

1518
01:22:51,425 --> 01:22:53,461
We want to show you
a clearer view.

1519
01:22:55,971 --> 01:22:57,006
Come on!

1520
01:23:12,821 --> 01:23:13,936
It looks like they've turned...

1521
01:23:14,031 --> 01:23:16,147
...onto the 59th Street Bridge.

1522
01:23:18,202 --> 01:23:19,783
As soon as we have them in sight...

1523
01:23:19,870 --> 01:23:21,656
...we'll bring you live footage.

1524
01:23:21,747 --> 01:23:22,747
LIVE

1525
01:23:27,211 --> 01:23:28,417
All right. Don't lose him.

1526
01:23:35,802 --> 01:23:37,008
Come on!

1527
01:23:41,058 --> 01:23:42,594
Behind the bus!

1528
01:23:46,188 --> 01:23:47,223
Get past it!

1529
01:23:50,817 --> 01:23:51,852
Shit!

1530
01:24:15,384 --> 01:24:16,384
Come here!

1531
01:24:17,678 --> 01:24:19,543
Back off!

1532
01:24:20,597 --> 01:24:21,882
Dylan, come on!

1533
01:24:22,057 --> 01:24:23,057
Come on!

1534
01:24:24,560 --> 01:24:26,221
Come on, man! Get out!

1535
01:24:26,728 --> 01:24:30,061
Leave him! There's
nothing you can do! Let's go! Leave him!

1536
01:24:34,903 --> 01:24:35,903
The paper!

1537
01:24:36,530 --> 01:24:38,441
Let's go!

1538
01:25:03,765 --> 01:25:07,974
Today, the popular and controversial story
of a group of magicians...

1539
01:25:08,103 --> 01:25:10,094
...who have captured
the public's imagination...

1540
01:25:10,188 --> 01:25:12,363
...took a dark turn
when a police operation...

1541
01:25:12,413 --> 01:25:13,521
...that began in Chinatown...

1542
01:25:13,609 --> 01:25:15,690
...ended in a dramatic...

1543
01:25:15,740 --> 01:25:17,397
...high-speed chase
across the 59th Street Bridge.

1544
01:25:17,487 --> 01:25:20,445
It caused a fatal crash
that claimed the life of Jack Wilder...

1545
01:25:20,532 --> 01:25:22,818
...one of the so-called Four Horsemen.

1546
01:25:24,036 --> 01:25:28,120
The whereabouts of the other three
Horsemen remain unknown.

1547
01:25:33,003 --> 01:25:35,665
Dylan, they've figured out
what the paper Jack was carrying was.

1548
01:25:35,756 --> 01:25:37,041
Do they know what it was?

1549
01:25:37,132 --> 01:25:38,838
And why he died
trying to protect it.

1550
01:25:38,925 --> 01:25:40,586
Evans is on his way
to brief us.

1551
01:25:40,677 --> 01:25:42,213
Let's go.

1552
01:25:42,304 --> 01:25:43,589
You'll want to take this call first.

1553
01:25:43,680 --> 01:25:44,680
Who is it?

1554
01:25:44,765 --> 01:25:47,006
Your friend Thaddeus Bradley.

1555
01:25:47,559 --> 01:25:48,765
Rhodes.

1556
01:25:48,894 --> 01:25:50,509
You understand the role...

1557
01:25:50,604 --> 01:25:52,720
...of the magician's assistant,
don't you, Agent Rhodes?

1558
01:25:53,065 --> 01:25:54,521
Yes, her job is to distract you...

1559
01:25:54,608 --> 01:25:55,768
...while he sets up the trick.

1560
01:25:57,027 --> 01:25:59,109
If you weren't so wrong,
you'd be right.

1561
01:25:59,529 --> 01:26:01,565
While you're watching the magician...

1562
01:26:02,074 --> 01:26:04,360
...the beautiful innocent assistant
is actually pulling the strings.

1563
01:26:04,576 --> 01:26:07,534
Don't you find it odd
that Interpol sent you...

1564
01:26:07,746 --> 01:26:11,238
...a rookie assistant
fresh out of the office?

1565
01:26:11,750 --> 01:26:13,911
Why did she ask to be assigned to the case?

1566
01:26:14,252 --> 01:26:16,208
She was just assigned to the case.

1567
01:26:17,214 --> 01:26:18,579
It was random.

1568
01:26:19,049 --> 01:26:22,086
Like choosing
a card at random from a deck.

1569
01:26:22,302 --> 01:26:27,012
I'm surprised by this generosity
on your part. What's in it for you?

1570
01:26:27,265 --> 01:26:29,096
I'm trying to level the playing field.

1571
01:26:29,393 --> 01:26:33,762
It's like playing against someone
wearing flippers and shackles.

1572
01:26:34,439 --> 01:26:38,227
Keep going, Agent Rhodes. Keep going.

1573
01:26:42,072 --> 01:26:43,072
Hey.

1574
01:26:43,448 --> 01:26:44,608
We've got a problem.

1575
01:26:45,826 --> 01:26:46,826
Here's the safe.

1576
01:26:46,993 --> 01:26:48,449
No. Hey.

1577
01:26:48,620 --> 01:26:49,780
What's he doing here?

1578
01:26:49,955 --> 01:26:51,570
He brought us
our first real lead.

1579
01:26:51,790 --> 01:26:55,749
We've been investigating Elkhorn,
a private security firm, for five years.

1580
01:26:55,750 --> 01:26:56,750
And?

1581
01:26:56,795 --> 01:26:58,456
They've got half a million
dollars hidden--

1582
01:26:58,630 --> 01:27:01,292
I wasn't asking you. I asked him.

1583
01:27:01,508 --> 01:27:03,794
The money's in a vault
in a warehouse in Queens.

1584
01:27:04,010 --> 01:27:06,467
We think it's
the magicians' final target.

1585
01:27:06,680 --> 01:27:08,466
The magician who died on the bridge...

1586
01:27:08,682 --> 01:27:12,345
...had our secret Elkhorn file
clutched in his burned little hand.

1587
01:27:12,602 --> 01:27:14,593
Despite all that
Robin Hood-style showmanship...

1588
01:27:14,813 --> 01:27:16,644
...those magicians are nothing
but common thieves.

1589
01:27:16,857 --> 01:27:18,313
How did they get the file?

1590
01:27:19,276 --> 01:27:22,313
Now comes the part
you'll find most interesting.

1591
01:27:22,487 --> 01:27:23,818
They connected to my server...

1592
01:27:24,030 --> 01:27:27,022
...after gaining access to your
mobile command center in New Orleans...

1593
01:27:27,284 --> 01:27:29,320
...using codes
they got from your phone.

1594
01:27:30,996 --> 01:27:33,032
You sent all of us chasing them.

1595
01:27:33,290 --> 01:27:35,872
The level of incompetence
displayed in this case...

1596
01:27:36,126 --> 01:27:38,117
...is a magic trick in itself.

1597
01:27:38,503 --> 01:27:41,336
Abracadabra. I'll take it from here.

1598
01:27:42,215 --> 01:27:44,331
Chief, is he taking over?

1599
01:27:44,676 --> 01:27:46,166
Come on, gentlemen.

1600
01:27:48,180 --> 01:27:49,545
Hey.

1601
01:27:49,639 --> 01:27:51,049
I want to talk to you.

1602
01:27:51,183 --> 01:27:52,343
Don't talk to me like that.

1603
01:27:52,434 --> 01:27:54,470
- Why the hell did you choose this case?
- What?

1604
01:27:54,561 --> 01:27:56,722
They say I'm "hard to read."
An American expression.

1605
01:27:56,813 --> 01:27:58,223
Do you understand it?

1606
01:27:58,315 --> 01:27:59,350
Yes.

1607
01:27:59,483 --> 01:28:01,690
Then I'll tell you
exactly what I think.

1608
01:28:01,818 --> 01:28:04,184
If I find out you're not...

1609
01:28:04,279 --> 01:28:05,564
...who you say you are--

1610
01:28:05,697 --> 01:28:07,483
We have another saying in French.

1611
01:28:09,326 --> 01:28:10,987
"Faith can move mountains."

1612
01:28:11,077 --> 01:28:12,738
If we're going to catch the Horsemen...

1613
01:28:12,829 --> 01:28:14,660
...you'll have to have a little faith in me...

1614
01:28:14,915 --> 01:28:16,906
...because I've done nothing
to deserve otherwise.

1615
01:28:17,000 --> 01:28:19,742
I just got my ass kicked.

1616
01:28:19,836 --> 01:28:21,326
I'm going to lose the case.

1617
01:28:21,463 --> 01:28:24,796
Faith is a luxury I can't afford.

1618
01:28:31,014 --> 01:28:33,221
More than anything in the world...

1619
01:28:33,308 --> 01:28:38,678
...Jack wanted to become
the most famous magician in history.

1620
01:28:38,772 --> 01:28:40,137
And I can't say he succeeded...

1621
01:28:40,232 --> 01:28:44,191
...but I hope wherever he is,
he's surrounded by magic.

1622
01:28:45,946 --> 01:28:48,278
What I'm trying to say is-- I'm sorry.

1623
01:28:48,365 --> 01:28:49,821
What I'm trying to say is...

1624
01:28:49,908 --> 01:28:53,890
The reason we're here is
to say that we can't...

1625
01:28:53,940 --> 01:28:56,120
We can't give up.

1626
01:28:56,206 --> 01:28:58,743
We started something
bigger than all of us.

1627
01:28:58,834 --> 01:29:00,665
We have to finish it.

1628
01:29:09,719 --> 01:29:10,799
We're approaching the warehouse.

1629
01:29:10,887 --> 01:29:13,378
Units five and six will cover
the north and south entrances.

1630
01:29:13,473 --> 01:29:14,713
We'll go straight to the vault.

1631
01:29:19,813 --> 01:29:22,725
Where's the safe?

1632
01:29:26,903 --> 01:29:27,903
What is this?

1633
01:29:27,988 --> 01:29:29,353
Where the hell is the safe?

1634
01:29:29,447 --> 01:29:31,312
We just moved it.

1635
01:29:31,408 --> 01:29:32,523
On Evans's orders.

1636
01:29:32,659 --> 01:29:33,694
I never said that.

1637
01:29:33,785 --> 01:29:35,116
They called from Washington...

1638
01:29:35,245 --> 01:29:36,826
...and told him to do it.

1639
01:29:36,913 --> 01:29:38,995
Those words never came out of my mouth.

1640
01:29:39,082 --> 01:29:42,453
What happened? Tell me what happened!
This is all nonsense!

1641
01:29:42,627 --> 01:29:43,742
No!

1642
01:29:49,467 --> 01:29:50,957
Oh, my God.

1643
01:29:51,136 --> 01:29:54,094
Sir? What are you doing?

1644
01:29:54,347 --> 01:29:55,655
It's Beethoven's concerto
in D major.

1645
01:29:55,705 --> 01:29:56,759
Where's the safe?

1646
01:29:56,975 --> 01:29:58,840
They're loading it onto the truck.

1647
01:30:02,647 --> 01:30:04,308
- Hey!
- Hey!

1648
01:30:05,859 --> 01:30:07,190
Stop the truck!

1649
01:30:08,028 --> 01:30:10,019
- Stop!
- Stop the truck!

1650
01:30:18,830 --> 01:30:20,821
What the hell's going on? We have orders.

1651
01:30:20,999 --> 01:30:22,489
Now you have new orders. Open it.

1652
01:30:26,504 --> 01:30:27,960
Here's what I want you to do.

1653
01:30:28,340 --> 01:30:30,797
Right now,
they think the safe is on its way.

1654
01:30:31,009 --> 01:30:34,217
So we're going to escort it to...
Where are they taking it?

1655
01:30:34,471 --> 01:30:35,551
To 5 Pointz, just ahead.

1656
01:30:35,805 --> 01:30:38,672
We'll escort it to 5 Pointz.
We're going wherever the safe goes.

1657
01:30:38,850 --> 01:30:40,340
And the safe will lead us to them.

1658
01:30:40,852 --> 01:30:42,058
You two come with me.

1659
01:30:54,824 --> 01:30:55,824
Guys...

1660
01:30:56,910 --> 01:30:59,492
Headquarters just called.

1661
01:30:59,704 --> 01:31:01,490
They uploaded this
to YouTube twenty minutes ago.

1662
01:31:01,748 --> 01:31:05,286
We're here to tell you
that we're not going to--

1663
01:31:05,585 --> 01:31:07,325
We can't quit now.

1664
01:31:07,587 --> 01:31:10,044
We started something
bigger than all of us.

1665
01:31:10,256 --> 01:31:11,712
We have to finish it.

1666
01:31:11,883 --> 01:31:14,340
Remember Jack Wilder's name
when you watch us live.

1667
01:31:14,552 --> 01:31:16,417
At 5 Pointz, Queens,
at seven o'clock.

1668
01:31:16,680 --> 01:31:19,012
That's in half an hour.
It's where we're headed.

1669
01:31:19,265 --> 01:31:21,381
I don't know, boss.
I don't want to sound pessimistic...

1670
01:31:21,559 --> 01:31:23,595
...but these guys are cheaters.

1671
01:31:23,853 --> 01:31:26,515
Rhodes, they're entertainers.

1672
01:31:28,775 --> 01:31:30,731
Here at 5 Pointz, we see
a crowd gathering...

1673
01:31:31,027 --> 01:31:34,861
...for the final showdown
between the fearless Horsemen and the FBI.

1674
01:31:35,073 --> 01:31:38,907
The Horsemen invited us
to their final battle with the FBI.

1675
01:31:39,077 --> 01:31:42,444
What do they have in store for us?
We're about to find out.

1676
01:31:53,800 --> 01:31:56,086
Everyone watch the truck.
Let's see who approaches.

1677
01:31:56,261 --> 01:31:57,291
Copy that.

1678
01:32:13,653 --> 01:32:15,439
Agents Rhodes and Fuller?

1679
01:32:16,281 --> 01:32:17,281
Ready.

1680
01:32:17,282 --> 01:32:19,773
We've got movement.
Someone's approaching the truck.

1681
01:32:19,993 --> 01:32:20,993
It's a woman.

1682
01:32:25,290 --> 01:32:27,281
- Freeze!
- Shit!

1683
01:32:28,251 --> 01:32:29,741
What the hell is this?

1684
01:32:30,795 --> 01:32:33,002
Hey! It's the guy from TV!

1685
01:32:33,256 --> 01:32:34,256
Lower your weapons.

1686
01:32:34,507 --> 01:32:37,340
Look who it is. The fifth Horseman.

1687
01:32:37,594 --> 01:32:40,631
Are you kidding? I'm following them,
just like you.

1688
01:32:40,805 --> 01:32:42,261
I'm not following anyone anymore.

1689
01:32:42,474 --> 01:32:43,805
You used to be ahead of us.

1690
01:32:43,975 --> 01:32:45,636
And now
I'm ahead of you.

1691
01:32:45,810 --> 01:32:47,471
I caught you.

1692
01:32:47,645 --> 01:32:49,431
You're still a bunch of idiots.

1693
01:32:49,647 --> 01:32:50,647
Oh, yeah?

1694
01:32:50,774 --> 01:32:54,016
This is where
Lionel Shrike used to practice.

1695
01:32:54,486 --> 01:32:56,317
I made my first show here.

1696
01:32:56,488 --> 01:32:59,321
If you want to look even worse...

1697
01:32:59,491 --> 01:33:00,947
...why don't you open that?

1698
01:33:01,159 --> 01:33:02,990
And while you're at it, Agent Rhodes...

1699
01:33:03,203 --> 01:33:06,866
...could you put on that look
of utter confusion again?

1700
01:33:07,123 --> 01:33:09,205
It'll look great on my DVD cover.

1701
01:33:10,668 --> 01:33:11,783
Open the safe.

1702
01:33:12,003 --> 01:33:13,033
Go ahead.

1703
01:33:17,967 --> 01:33:19,082
What's this?

1704
01:33:21,137 --> 01:33:22,137
What?

1705
01:33:22,388 --> 01:33:23,468
Give me that.

1706
01:33:23,681 --> 01:33:24,681
No.

1707
01:33:25,183 --> 01:33:26,343
No!

1708
01:33:30,522 --> 01:33:31,557
What is this?

1709
01:33:32,524 --> 01:33:33,639
Back up!

1710
01:33:41,366 --> 01:33:43,857
Who doesn't love
a good magic trick?

1711
01:33:44,035 --> 01:33:46,993
Once again,
chasing empty safes.

1712
01:33:47,705 --> 01:33:50,913
Misdirection again.

1713
01:33:57,882 --> 01:33:59,372
The show's starting. Move!

1714
01:33:59,592 --> 01:34:02,379
The question I'd be asking myself right now,
Agent Rhodes...

1715
01:34:02,554 --> 01:34:04,385
...is what happened to the real one.

1716
01:34:25,368 --> 01:34:27,279
Come on! Clear the area!

1717
01:34:27,537 --> 01:34:29,073
Move the crowd back.

1718
01:34:30,206 --> 01:34:32,071
FBI! Make way!

1719
01:34:32,709 --> 01:34:34,290
Move!

1720
01:34:34,544 --> 01:34:35,909
What is magic?

1721
01:34:36,754 --> 01:34:39,621
Magic is deception,
but it's meant to delight...

1722
01:34:39,883 --> 01:34:41,544
...to entertain, to inspire.

1723
01:34:41,759 --> 01:34:44,045
Watch out! Make way!

1724
01:34:44,429 --> 01:34:45,544
It's a matter of conviction.

1725
01:34:45,638 --> 01:34:46,638
Of faith.

1726
01:34:46,764 --> 01:34:47,764
Of trust.

1727
01:34:51,769 --> 01:34:56,638
Without those qualities,
the art of magic wouldn't exist.

1728
01:34:57,233 --> 01:34:59,064
But what happens if those qualities...

1729
01:34:59,277 --> 01:35:01,233
...aren't used for the greater good?

1730
01:35:01,446 --> 01:35:04,233
If they're used to lie, to cheat...

1731
01:35:04,449 --> 01:35:06,565
For personal gain or out of greed.

1732
01:35:06,826 --> 01:35:08,441
Then it stops being magic.

1733
01:35:08,661 --> 01:35:09,741
And it becomes a crime.

1734
01:35:09,954 --> 01:35:12,991
Tonight, in our final act...

1735
01:35:13,249 --> 01:35:16,082
...you'll help us
set a few things right.

1736
01:35:20,006 --> 01:35:21,121
What's all this, Rhodes?

1737
01:35:21,424 --> 01:35:22,584
I don't know.

1738
01:35:23,635 --> 01:35:24,841
Let the show begin.

1739
01:35:29,974 --> 01:35:30,974
Look!

1740
01:35:35,313 --> 01:35:37,474
They're on the roof! Let's go!

1741
01:35:37,690 --> 01:35:38,975
Come on!

1742
01:35:39,359 --> 01:35:40,644
Dylan!

1743
01:35:42,278 --> 01:35:43,278
What are you doing here?

1744
01:35:43,446 --> 01:35:45,653
They're heading in the wrong direction,
and you know it.

1745
01:35:46,032 --> 01:35:48,364
Rhodes! I need every agent here!

1746
01:35:48,618 --> 01:35:51,951
Trust me. I need your blind faith.

1747
01:35:52,622 --> 01:35:53,657
Wait.

1748
01:35:54,123 --> 01:35:55,238
I'm going with her.

1749
01:35:55,458 --> 01:35:56,698
Go. I'll cover you. I'll wait here.

1750
01:35:58,544 --> 01:35:59,579
Go ahead!

1751
01:35:59,671 --> 01:36:01,161
It's your responsibility, Fuller.

1752
01:36:16,562 --> 01:36:20,601
Come on! Hurry!

1753
01:36:31,703 --> 01:36:32,783
Daniel?

1754
01:36:33,454 --> 01:36:34,454
Yes?

1755
01:36:34,789 --> 01:36:39,374
Now that our year
of living dangerously...

1756
01:36:39,627 --> 01:36:42,960
...is coming to an end,
in a moment of vulnerability...

1757
01:36:43,131 --> 01:36:47,500
...I'd like to tell you how I feel
about our relationship.

1758
01:36:47,760 --> 01:36:48,760
Okay.

1759
01:36:49,137 --> 01:36:53,255
When I met you,
I thought you were kind of a...

1760
01:36:54,642 --> 01:36:55,802
Jerk.

1761
01:36:57,478 --> 01:36:58,478
And?

1762
01:36:59,856 --> 01:37:01,517
No, that's it.

1763
01:37:01,774 --> 01:37:04,311
- That's lovely. Your words touched me.
- Yeah.

1764
01:37:04,527 --> 01:37:05,983
Yeah. Deeply.

1765
01:37:06,154 --> 01:37:07,769
Well, I didn't say where they touched me.

1766
01:37:07,989 --> 01:37:08,989
You little rascal!

1767
01:37:12,493 --> 01:37:14,199
God. What are we going to do
when all this is over?

1768
01:37:14,454 --> 01:37:17,662
We'll be free. The instructions
end after this show.

1769
01:37:18,166 --> 01:37:19,952
Even if the Eye doesn't exist...

1770
01:37:20,168 --> 01:37:24,002
...even if this was all a hoax
and we spend twenty years in prison...

1771
01:37:24,505 --> 01:37:26,166
...I just want to say that...

1772
01:37:26,466 --> 01:37:28,832
I know. Me too.

1773
01:37:30,136 --> 01:37:32,092
A little too sentimental for my taste.

1774
01:37:39,228 --> 01:37:40,343
There they are!

1775
01:37:43,149 --> 01:37:45,856
Get them! Come on!

1776
01:37:46,069 --> 01:37:47,069
Get them!

1777
01:37:52,033 --> 01:37:53,033
Get up!

1778
01:37:53,534 --> 01:37:54,694
What's going on?

1779
01:37:58,498 --> 01:37:59,658
Shit!

1780
01:38:08,883 --> 01:38:10,373
Hello, New York!

1781
01:38:18,601 --> 01:38:20,557
Thank you for the magic.

1782
01:38:20,728 --> 01:38:24,937
And thank you for being
such an incredible, devoted audience.

1783
01:38:25,358 --> 01:38:29,226
Unfortunately, like all good things,
this must come to an end.

1784
01:38:31,948 --> 01:38:34,109
We'd like to begin
tonight's performance...

1785
01:38:34,534 --> 01:38:36,070
...by saying goodbye.

1786
01:38:37,412 --> 01:38:40,700
All we wanted was to bring the world
into a magic show.

1787
01:38:40,915 --> 01:38:43,622
And in doing so, bring a little magic into the world.

1788
01:38:47,630 --> 01:38:48,745
Make way!

1789
01:38:58,558 --> 01:39:01,766
It's been an incredible experience
for all of us.

1790
01:39:02,270 --> 01:39:04,602
But the time has come for us to disappear.

1791
01:39:04,814 --> 01:39:06,145
Good night, New York.

1792
01:39:06,399 --> 01:39:08,435
And thank you for believing in us.

1793
01:39:10,445 --> 01:39:11,560
Stop!

1794
01:39:15,116 --> 01:39:16,116
Freeze!

1795
01:39:16,951 --> 01:39:18,157
No!

1796
01:39:35,803 --> 01:39:37,043
Damn it!

1797
01:40:05,833 --> 01:40:07,039
I'm sorry.

1798
01:40:07,710 --> 01:40:09,701
You were right. It was bigger than all of us.

1799
01:40:10,671 --> 01:40:14,755
For once I'm not happy
I was right.

1800
01:40:17,094 --> 01:40:18,709
I guess it's over.

1801
01:40:20,765 --> 01:40:21,880
You think so?

1802
01:40:48,042 --> 01:40:49,327
Dylan!

1803
01:40:49,710 --> 01:40:52,167
Dylan!

1804
01:40:52,255 --> 01:40:53,255
Hello.

1805
01:40:54,632 --> 01:40:56,588
- See this?
- What?

1806
01:41:00,221 --> 01:41:01,586
Where are the real bills?

1807
01:41:17,822 --> 01:41:21,781
You're under arrest! Hands up!

1808
01:41:31,460 --> 01:41:32,460
UPDATE

1809
01:41:32,837 --> 01:41:34,828
BREAKING NEWS
THE FOUR HORSEMEN ESCAPE THE FBI

1810
01:42:12,877 --> 01:42:13,992
I've been framed.

1811
01:42:15,504 --> 01:42:16,504
Sure you have.

1812
01:42:16,589 --> 01:42:17,874
Yes, I can prove it.

1813
01:42:18,215 --> 01:42:19,375
I always can.

1814
01:42:19,884 --> 01:42:21,715
Just like I predicted
you'd end up looking like a fool.

1815
01:42:21,927 --> 01:42:24,418
Let's drop the theatrics, shall we?

1816
01:42:24,680 --> 01:42:26,841
There aren't any cameras rolling here.

1817
01:42:27,058 --> 01:42:29,720
Can you give me a couple of minutes
alone with my friend?

1818
01:42:29,894 --> 01:42:30,924
Of course.

1819
01:42:36,567 --> 01:42:37,567
Very well.

1820
01:42:38,611 --> 01:42:41,569
I have information to offer.
That's how this works, right?

1821
01:42:41,739 --> 01:42:43,354
If the information is valuable.

1822
01:42:45,868 --> 01:42:48,610
Those bars
give you time to think.

1823
01:42:50,206 --> 01:42:53,414
I'm almost certain
I know how they did it.

1824
01:42:54,126 --> 01:42:56,242
Remember the rabbit trick
on the second night.

1825
01:42:57,213 --> 01:42:59,044
The rabbit box was never empty.

1826
01:43:01,133 --> 01:43:02,589
Neither was the warehouse.

1827
01:43:03,969 --> 01:43:06,381
After breaking into the warehouse...

1828
01:43:06,597 --> 01:43:08,553
...the Horsemen left
the real safe...

1829
01:43:08,766 --> 01:43:10,882
...and transformed the entire space...

1830
01:43:11,143 --> 01:43:13,634
...into one giant box
like the rabbit's.

1831
01:43:15,314 --> 01:43:17,475
You barged in...

1832
01:43:17,733 --> 01:43:20,145
...and thought the safe
was already gone.

1833
01:43:20,334 --> 01:43:21,335
Hey!

1834
01:43:21,445 --> 01:43:22,776
Stop!

1835
01:43:22,947 --> 01:43:24,403
Once again, the trick fooled you.

1836
01:43:24,615 --> 01:43:27,277
The idea was to make you believe
the safe was gone...

1837
01:43:27,493 --> 01:43:29,779
...so you'd let your guard down
and leave.

1838
01:43:30,162 --> 01:43:33,120
While you were playing
with balloon animals...

1839
01:43:33,332 --> 01:43:35,948
...someone went into the warehouse.

1840
01:43:54,478 --> 01:43:55,508
Who?

1841
01:43:57,022 --> 01:43:58,512
Jack Wilder.

1842
01:43:59,316 --> 01:44:00,647
Shit.

1843
01:44:00,818 --> 01:44:03,605
No. That's impossible.
He died right in front of me.

1844
01:44:03,988 --> 01:44:06,104
Unless he didn't die.

1845
01:44:13,456 --> 01:44:14,696
It's locked.

1846
01:44:15,499 --> 01:44:16,989
Haven't you learned anything?

1847
01:44:17,209 --> 01:44:18,665
Anything can be opened.

1848
01:44:19,670 --> 01:44:22,457
Well done, Mr. Wilder!
Nice work.

1849
01:44:22,673 --> 01:44:24,459
You're a real man now, Jack.

1850
01:44:24,675 --> 01:44:26,961
He escaped in an FBI vehicle, right?

1851
01:44:30,848 --> 01:44:32,179
You followed him to the bridge.

1852
01:44:32,391 --> 01:44:36,009
The other Horsemen were waiting there
with an identical vehicle.

1853
01:44:37,021 --> 01:44:39,137
In a split second,
you lost sight of him...

1854
01:44:39,356 --> 01:44:40,687
...and they switched cars.

1855
01:45:02,546 --> 01:45:05,538
When you climbed into
the burning car...

1856
01:45:05,883 --> 01:45:07,874
...very heroically,
I might add...

1857
01:45:09,762 --> 01:45:12,048
...you almost died trying to rescue...

1858
01:45:12,348 --> 01:45:15,340
...what I believe
was a body from the morgue.

1859
01:45:16,435 --> 01:45:20,178
Why would they go through
such an elaborate, dangerous plan...

1860
01:45:21,023 --> 01:45:22,513
...just to frame you?

1861
01:45:22,733 --> 01:45:25,270
- I don't know.
- They didn't keep the money.

1862
01:45:25,528 --> 01:45:27,860
That's the part I still don't understand.

1863
01:45:28,072 --> 01:45:30,529
But I've just given you
much more important information.

1864
01:45:30,783 --> 01:45:32,523
- Actually, no.
- What?

1865
01:45:32,743 --> 01:45:37,237
I finally got the chance
to investigate Lionel Shrike.

1866
01:45:37,456 --> 01:45:40,744
His triumphant comeback. The accident.

1867
01:45:41,043 --> 01:45:44,535
The insurance company
that refused to pay his family?

1868
01:45:44,755 --> 01:45:46,416
Tressler Insurance.

1869
01:45:47,925 --> 01:45:50,132
The bank that sold him the policy?

1870
01:45:51,220 --> 01:45:52,926
Crédit Républicain in Paris.

1871
01:45:53,097 --> 01:45:54,712
Crédit Républicain de Paris.

1872
01:45:56,100 --> 01:45:57,385
What do you think?

1873
01:45:57,601 --> 01:45:59,557
There's a sucker born every minute.

1874
01:46:00,479 --> 01:46:02,936
What if this whole thing was just...

1875
01:46:03,148 --> 01:46:05,890
...to get us mugged
in Central Park at two in the morning?

1876
01:46:06,110 --> 01:46:08,271
No, I'm telling you we're
exactly where we're supposed to be.

1877
01:46:08,779 --> 01:46:09,779
We just need to find--

1878
01:46:09,947 --> 01:46:10,947
That?

1879
01:46:13,576 --> 01:46:15,282
Lionel Shrike's tree.

1880
01:46:16,245 --> 01:46:18,236
And the card inside the glass.

1881
01:46:20,124 --> 01:46:21,580
What do we do now?

1882
01:46:38,642 --> 01:46:39,642
Wow!

1883
01:46:55,826 --> 01:46:57,362
I've got a new theory.

1884
01:46:57,620 --> 01:47:01,283
According to legend,
the Eye is everywhere.

1885
01:47:01,707 --> 01:47:02,707
Waiting...

1886
01:47:03,292 --> 01:47:07,661
...for the truly great magicians
to distinguish themselves...

1887
01:47:08,005 --> 01:47:09,791
...from the mediocre ones.

1888
01:47:10,716 --> 01:47:12,297
Maybe that's what you were.

1889
01:47:12,509 --> 01:47:15,842
Deep down, all you wanted
was to join the order...

1890
01:47:16,013 --> 01:47:17,503
...but they never invited you.

1891
01:47:17,723 --> 01:47:20,931
So you tried to destroy it.
And what happened?

1892
01:47:21,185 --> 01:47:23,016
You made them angry.

1893
01:47:24,355 --> 01:47:27,518
The Eye doesn't exist.

1894
01:47:27,691 --> 01:47:30,774
Then tell me
who's behind all this.

1895
01:47:31,028 --> 01:47:33,690
- A meticulous man with an obsession.
- Who?

1896
01:47:33,906 --> 01:47:37,069
Someone willing to sacrifice everything.

1897
01:47:37,326 --> 01:47:39,863
Someone so prepared to lose...

1898
01:47:40,037 --> 01:47:42,323
...that he wouldn't even become a suspect
until the trick was over.

1899
01:47:42,581 --> 01:47:45,414
I don't want a profile,
I want a name. Who?

1900
01:47:45,668 --> 01:47:47,329
I don't know.

1901
01:47:47,544 --> 01:47:51,162
But he had access to the warehouse
to install the mirrors.

1902
01:47:52,508 --> 01:47:56,421
He was always one step ahead of me.
And the FBI too.

1903
01:47:56,679 --> 01:47:59,921
He didn't evade them once or twice,
but every single time.

1904
01:48:00,182 --> 01:48:02,173
Almost as if he were inside--

1905
01:48:15,572 --> 01:48:17,187
Inside.

1906
01:48:29,086 --> 01:48:30,121
You.

1907
01:48:34,800 --> 01:48:35,960
Why?

1908
01:48:36,552 --> 01:48:38,634
That's the question, isn't it?

1909
01:48:39,722 --> 01:48:40,757
You're right.

1910
01:48:41,890 --> 01:48:44,097
You have no idea how long I've waited...

1911
01:48:44,309 --> 01:48:46,265
...to see that look on your face.

1912
01:48:48,772 --> 01:48:50,103
Who are you?

1913
01:48:51,316 --> 01:48:52,931
What do you want from me?

1914
01:48:53,610 --> 01:48:55,100
What do I want?

1915
01:48:55,487 --> 01:48:58,274
For you to spend the rest of your life
in this cage...

1916
01:48:58,490 --> 01:49:02,483
...staring at the walls and wondering
how you didn't see it.

1917
01:49:03,245 --> 01:49:06,578
How you let yourself be so blinded
by your own ego...

1918
01:49:06,832 --> 01:49:09,118
...that you convinced yourself
you had the upper hand...

1919
01:49:09,334 --> 01:49:11,666
...when you were actually
losing the race.

1920
01:49:16,759 --> 01:49:17,839
Dylan?

1921
01:49:18,594 --> 01:49:19,959
One minute.

1922
01:49:20,971 --> 01:49:23,132
Dylan! How did this happen?

1923
01:49:32,608 --> 01:49:33,608
Why?

1924
01:49:34,818 --> 01:49:35,978
Why?

1925
01:49:39,364 --> 01:49:41,480
Oh, my God.

1926
01:49:42,493 --> 01:49:44,449
I didn't see that coming.

1927
01:49:44,661 --> 01:49:46,652
- It's impossible!
- It can't be.

1928
01:49:46,872 --> 01:49:49,784
Honestly, that was impressive.

1929
01:49:50,000 --> 01:49:51,000
Thank you.

1930
01:49:51,210 --> 01:49:53,667
When I said, "Always be
the smartest person in the room"...

1931
01:49:53,879 --> 01:49:54,959
We agreed.

1932
01:49:55,172 --> 01:49:56,457
Of course. Wow.

1933
01:49:56,673 --> 01:49:57,788
Henley.

1934
01:49:58,300 --> 01:50:00,006
I've never seen her speechless.

1935
01:50:00,219 --> 01:50:02,130
That's high praise.

1936
01:50:02,346 --> 01:50:05,554
Man, I owe you an apology
for punching you.

1937
01:50:05,849 --> 01:50:07,305
Hey, for the record...

1938
01:50:07,518 --> 01:50:10,931
...I've always been
a hundred percent believer.

1939
01:50:11,188 --> 01:50:15,352
All the energy I spent
keeping these nonbelievers in line--

1940
01:50:15,692 --> 01:50:17,557
Merritt, you're in.

1941
01:50:18,570 --> 01:50:19,605
Thank you.

1942
01:50:19,863 --> 01:50:20,863
Come on.

1943
01:50:21,865 --> 01:50:25,028
The real magic is taking
four such great solo acts...

1944
01:50:25,202 --> 01:50:27,067
...and making them work together.

1945
01:50:27,329 --> 01:50:29,445
And that's exactly
what you did.

1946
01:50:31,041 --> 01:50:32,326
Welcome.

1947
01:50:35,212 --> 01:50:36,702
Welcome to the Eye.

1948
01:51:35,772 --> 01:51:38,935
TRAPPED IN A WATERY GRAVE

1949
01:51:41,612 --> 01:51:44,274
The River Claims the Great Shrike
The Great Trick Ends in Tragedy

1950
01:51:44,489 --> 01:51:45,979
Everything was connected.

1951
01:51:46,950 --> 01:51:49,942
Crédit Républicain in Paris.
Tressler Insurance.

1952
01:51:59,338 --> 01:52:02,671
And Elkhorn?
What's its connection to Shrike?

1953
01:52:05,302 --> 01:52:08,465
Elkhorn started out
as a safe manufacturer.

1954
01:52:08,680 --> 01:52:10,796
It used poor-quality steel.

1955
01:52:12,434 --> 01:52:13,594
It cut corners.

1956
01:52:13,810 --> 01:52:16,847
When the safe hit the riverbed,
it buckled.

1957
01:52:17,105 --> 01:52:18,641
And he was trapped inside.

1958
01:52:19,983 --> 01:52:21,689
He was your father.

1959
01:52:23,528 --> 01:52:26,861
The morning before the show
seemed like any other day.

1960
01:52:27,908 --> 01:52:30,065
When my father said
he was leaving for work...

1961
01:52:30,115 --> 01:52:32,902
...he could see I didn't want him to go.

1962
01:52:35,165 --> 01:52:40,285
So he gave me his watch and told me
to count the seconds until he came back.

1963
01:52:41,463 --> 01:52:44,170
Since then, I've never let
the watch stop.

1964
01:52:44,258 --> 01:52:46,374
And from that moment on...

1965
01:52:46,468 --> 01:52:49,005
...I created a character.

1966
01:52:49,638 --> 01:52:51,549
I made plans
and prepared every last detail.

1967
01:52:51,640 --> 01:52:54,928
But the one thing
I never imagined could happen...

1968
01:52:55,352 --> 01:52:56,512
...was you coming into my life.

1969
01:52:58,188 --> 01:53:01,146
And I know the logical conclusion for you...

1970
01:53:01,650 --> 01:53:04,392
...would be to bring the case
to its conclusion...

1971
01:53:04,486 --> 01:53:07,523
...and turn me in.

1972
01:53:12,661 --> 01:53:13,696
Yes.

1973
01:53:15,664 --> 01:53:19,498
But you know how I feel
about logic and conclusions.

1974
01:53:20,877 --> 01:53:22,208
And you also know...

1975
01:53:23,088 --> 01:53:27,422
...that some things
are better left unexplained.

1976
01:53:33,932 --> 01:53:35,718
One more secret to keep.

1977
01:54:03,253 --> 01:54:04,459
Come closer.

1978
01:54:05,255 --> 01:54:06,255
Closer.

1979
01:54:06,757 --> 01:54:08,668
Because now you know our secret.

1980
01:54:08,967 --> 01:54:11,299
We could be anywhere,
watching you.

1981
01:54:11,970 --> 01:54:14,962
We're looking for someone to help us
with our next trick.

1982
01:54:15,599 --> 01:54:19,808
On the count of three,
open your eyes and tell me what you see.

1983
01:54:21,146 --> 01:54:22,146
One...

1984
01:54:22,356 --> 01:54:23,356
...two...

1985
01:55:55,282 --> 01:55:57,147
Please, stop.

1986
01:55:57,534 --> 01:56:01,243
Stop the van. Stop.
I'm going to jump out of the van.

1987
01:56:02,247 --> 01:56:03,703
Thank God.

1988
01:56:05,667 --> 01:56:07,749
Where are we?

1989
01:56:07,836 --> 01:56:10,202
Great. Another middle of nowhere.

1990
01:56:10,338 --> 01:56:12,203
Wow, I can feel it.

1991
01:56:12,340 --> 01:56:16,504
I'm exhausted, hungry,
and in a bad mood.

1992
01:56:17,554 --> 01:56:19,419
Look at this place.

1993
01:56:19,514 --> 01:56:22,301
- Where did you bring us?
- Weren't we already in a dump?

1994
01:56:22,392 --> 01:56:24,257
This place is great.

1995
01:56:25,812 --> 01:56:26,812
Is this our new equipment?

1996
01:56:26,897 --> 01:56:28,433
They'd better not be matching outfits.

1997
01:56:28,523 --> 01:56:29,558
I don't wear tight clothes.

1998
01:56:29,649 --> 01:56:32,015
- It's locked.
- Guys, the cards.

1999
01:56:32,110 --> 01:56:34,647
- What cards?
- What do you mean, "what cards"?

2000
01:56:38,742 --> 01:56:39,742
All right.

2001
01:56:40,710 --> 01:56:42,460
Subtitles by aRGENTeaM
www.argenteam.net
